(function () {
  function P(q, a, h, s) { return { q: q, a: a, h: h || "", s: s || "" }; }

  var SSC_JE_ENC_THERMO = {};
SSC_JE_ENC_THERMO.thermo = [
      P("What is the absolute zero temperature in Celsius?", "−273.15 °C", "Triple point of water defines 0.01 °C.", "Absolute zero is the lowest possible temperature where all thermal motion ceases. The triple point of water is defined as 0.01 °C (273.16 K) and serves as a fixed point for the Kelvin scale. Absolute zero equals −273.15 °C or 0 K."),
      P("What is the relation between Celsius and Fahrenheit scales?", "°F = (9/5)°C + 32", "Two fixed points: ice point 32 °F, steam point 212 °F.", "The Fahrenheit scale sets the freezing point of water at 32 °F and boiling at 212 °F, giving 180 divisions. The Celsius scale has 100 divisions between the same two points. Hence °F = (9/5)°C + 32 and °C = (5/9)(°F − 32)."),
      P("What is the Rankine temperature scale?", "°R = °F + 459.67", "Rankine is the absolute scale on Fahrenheit basis.", "Rankine is the absolute temperature scale corresponding to Fahrenheit. T(R) = T(°F) + 459.67. It is used mainly in American engineering practice. 0 °R corresponds to absolute zero."),
      P("State the Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics.", "If A and C and B and C are each in thermal equilibrium, then A and B are in thermal equilibrium", "Thermal equilibrium is transitive.", "If two systems are each in thermal equilibrium with a third system, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other. This law establishes the concept of temperature as a measurable property and justifies the use of thermometers."),
      P("What is a thermodynamic system?", "A quantity of matter or a region in space chosen for study", "Surroundings plus system form the universe.", "A thermodynamic system is the specific portion of the universe being analyzed. Everything outside the system is called the surroundings. The boundary separates the system from the surroundings. Systems can be open, closed, or isolated."),
      P("Distinguish between a closed system and an open system.", "Closed: mass fixed, energy may cross; Open: both mass and energy cross the boundary", "Open system is also called a control volume.", "A closed system (control mass) contains a fixed amount of matter and exchanges only energy through its boundary. An open system (control volume) allows both mass and energy to cross its boundary. An isolated system exchanges neither mass nor energy."),
      P("What is a thermodynamic process?", "Any change of a system from one equilibrium state to another", "A quasi-static process is an internal equilibrium idealization.", "A thermodynamic process describes the path of a system from one equilibrium state to another. In a quasi-static (quasi-equilibrium) process the system passes through a continuous series of equilibrium states. This idealized path is useful for analysis."),
      P("What is a quasi-static process?", "An infinitely slow process during which the system stays in equilibrium at every step", "Frictionless and internally reversible idealization.", "A quasi-static process is carried out so slowly that the system remains infinitesimally close to equilibrium at all times. It requires a vanishingly small driving imbalance at each step. In the absence of friction and other dissipations it is reversible."),
      P("What is the difference between a path function and a point function?", "Path functions depend on the path (Q, W); point functions depend only on the state (P, V, T, U, H, S)", "Point functions have exact differentials.", "Point functions (properties) depend only on the state of the system, not on the way it was reached; their differentials are exact. Path functions such as heat Q and work W depend on the process path followed, so their differentials are inexact."),
      P("State the First Law of Thermodynamics for a closed system.", "Q − W = ΔU", "Energy conservation for a closed system.", "The first law states that energy is conserved. For a closed system the net heat transferred in minus the net work done out equals the change in internal energy: Q − W = ΔU. For a cyclic process Q = W. Internal energy U is a state function."),
      P("What is internal energy of an ideal gas?", "It is a function of temperature only: U = f(T)", "For an ideal gas U depends only on T.", "For an ideal gas, internal energy is a function of temperature only: ΔU = m·Cv·ΔT. It does not depend on pressure or volume. This is Joule's result and follows from the ideal-gas equation of state together with the first law."),
      P("Define enthalpy.", "H = U + PV", "Enthalpy is convenient for steady-flow processes.", "Enthalpy is defined as H = U + PV, where U is internal energy, P is pressure, and V is volume. For an ideal gas enthalpy is also a function of temperature only: ΔH = m·Cp·ΔT. Enthalpy simplifies the energy balance of steady-flow devices."),
      P("What is Cp for air at standard conditions?", "1.005 kJ/kg·K", "Cp = Cv + R for an ideal gas.", "The specific heat at constant pressure for air is Cp = 1.005 kJ/kg·K. For an ideal gas, Cp − Cv = R. Since R = 0.287 kJ/kg·K for air, Cv = 1.005 − 0.287 = 0.718 kJ/kg·K. These values are the standard ones in air-standard analysis."),
      P("What is Cv for air at standard conditions?", "0.718 kJ/kg·K", "Cv = Cp − R = 1.005 − 0.287.", "The specific heat at constant volume for air is Cv = 0.718 kJ/kg·K. It relates the internal-energy change to the temperature change: ΔU = m·Cv·ΔT. This value is used for constant-volume and closed-system processes."),
      P("What is the characteristic gas constant R for air?", "R = 0.287 kJ/kg·K", "R = Ru/M = 8.314/28.97.", "The characteristic gas constant for air is R = Ru/M, where Ru = 8.314 kJ/kmol·K is the universal gas constant and M ≈ 28.97 kg/kmol is the molar mass of dry air. Hence R ≈ 8.314/28.97 ≈ 0.287 kJ/kg·K."),
      P("What is the relation Cp − Cv = R?", "Cp − Cv = R per unit mass of an ideal gas", "Holds for the specific heats of any ideal gas.", "For an ideal gas the difference between the specific heats at constant pressure and constant volume equals the gas constant: Cp − Cv = R. On a molar basis, Cp,m − Cv,m = Ru = 8.314 kJ/kmol·K. This follows from H = U + PV and the ideal-gas law."),
      P("What is the ratio of specific heats γ for air?", "γ = Cp/Cv = 1.4", "γ = 1.4 for diatomic gases such as air.", "The ratio of specific heats is γ = Cp/Cv. For air (a diatomic gas) γ = 1.005/0.718 ≈ 1.4. This ratio appears in isentropic relations such as PV^γ = constant and in air-standard cycle efficiencies."),
      P("What is the reversible boundary work for a closed system?", "W = ∫P dV", "It is the area under the curve on a P-V diagram.", "For a quasi-static (reversible) process in a closed system the boundary work is W = ∫P dV, evaluated between the end states. It equals the area under the process curve on a P-V diagram. Different reversible paths between the same states give different work values."),
      P("What is the work done in a constant-pressure (isobaric) process?", "W = P(V₂ − V₁)", "For an ideal gas this equals mR(T₂ − T₁).", "At constant pressure, W = ∫P dV = P(V₂ − V₁). For an ideal gas, using PV = mRT, this becomes W = mR(T₂ − T₁). The heat transfer for a closed gas is Q = mCp(T₂ − T₁)."),
      P("What is the work done in a constant-volume (isochoric) process?", "W = 0", "No volume change means no boundary work.", "At constant volume dV = 0, so W = ∫P dV = 0. All heat added changes the internal energy: Q = ΔU = mCv(T₂ − T₁). This is typical of a rigid tank and of the combustion stroke of the Otto cycle."),
      P("What is the work done in an isothermal process for an ideal gas?", "W = mRT ln(V₂/V₁) = mRT ln(P₁/P₂)", "Temperature remains constant throughout.", "For an isothermal process of an ideal gas, PV = constant, so W = ∫P dV = mRT ln(V₂/V₁). By the first law ΔU = 0 for an ideal gas at constant T, hence Q = W. The pressure-ratio form follows from P₁V₁ = P₂V₂."),
      P("What is the work done in a polytropic process PV^n = const for n ≠ 1?", "W = (P₁V₁ − P₂V₂)/(n − 1) = mR(T₁ − T₂)/(n − 1)", "The polytropic index n characterizes the path.", "For a polytropic process PV^n = C with n ≠ 1, integrating ∫P dV gives W = (P₁V₁ − P₂V₂)/(n − 1). Using the gas law this equals mR(T₁ − T₂)/(n − 1). For n = 1 it reduces to the isothermal result W = mRT ln(V₂/V₁)."),
      P("What is the work done in an adiabatic process for an ideal gas?", "W = mR(T₁ − T₂)/(γ − 1) = (P₁V₁ − P₂V₂)/(γ − 1)", "Adiabatic: Q = 0 and n = γ.", "For a reversible adiabatic (isentropic) process, PV^γ = constant and Q = 0. The work is W = (P₁V₁ − P₂V₂)/(γ − 1) = mCv(T₁ − T₂). By the first law W = −ΔU. Entropy remains constant during a reversible adiabatic process."),
      P("Write the Steady-Flow Energy Equation (SFEE).", "Q̇ − Ẇ = ṁ[(h₂ − h₁) + (V₂² − V₁²)/2 + g(z₂ − z₁)]", "Energy balance for a steady-flow control volume.", "At steady state, energy entering with the stream is ṁ[h₁ + V₁²/2 + gz₁] + Q̇ and leaving with the stream plus work is ṁ[h₂ + V₂²/2 + gz₂] + Ẇ. Equating these recovers the SFEE, which is the basis for analyzing nozzles, turbines, compressors, boilers and throttling valves."),
      P("Write the energy equation for a boiler.", "Q̇ = ṁ(h₂ − h₁)", "No work, negligible KE and PE changes.", "A boiler is a steady-flow device with no work interaction; kinetic and potential energy changes are negligible. The SFEE therefore reduces to Q̇ = ṁ(h₂ − h₁), where h₁ is the feedwater enthalpy at inlet and h₂ is the steam enthalpy at exit."),
      P("Write the energy equation for a nozzle.", "h₁ + V₁²/2 = h₂ + V₂²/2", "Adiabatic, no shaft work, PE neglected.", "A nozzle accelerates fluid without heat or shaft work, and potential-energy change is negligible. The SFEE reduces to h₁ + V₁²/2 = h₂ + V₂²/2, giving exit speed V₂ = √[2(h₁ − h₂)] when the inlet velocity is negligible."),
      P("Write the energy equation for a turbine.", "Ẇ = ṁ(h₁ − h₂)", "No heat transfer, negligible KE and PE changes.", "A turbine produces shaft work from the flow. For an adiabatic (or near-adiabatic) machine with negligible kinetic and potential energy changes the SFEE gives Ẇ = ṁ(h₁ − h₂). The maximum work for a given inlet state occurs in an isentropic expansion to the exit pressure."),
      P("Write the energy equation for a compressor.", "Ẇ = ṁ(h₂ − h₁)", "Work input raises the fluid pressure.", "A compressor requires shaft work input to raise the fluid pressure. Ignoring heat loss and KE/PE changes, the SFEE gives Ẇ = ṁ(h₂ − h₁). With isentropic efficiency η_c, the actual work is Ẇ_actual = Ẇ_isentropic/η_c."),
      P("Write the energy equation for a throttling valve.", "h₁ = h₂ (isenthalpic)", "No work, no heat, negligible KE and PE changes.", "Throttling is an isenthalpic process in which enthalpy remains constant. The SFEE has zero work, zero heat, and negligible kinetic/potential changes, so h₁ = h₂. Pressure drops; the temperature change is governed by the Joule–Thomson coefficient."),
      P("What is the Joule–Thomson coefficient?", "μJT = (∂T/∂P)_h", "Positive μJT means cooling on expansion.", "The Joule–Thomson coefficient μJT = (∂T/∂P)_h measures the temperature change of a fluid during an isenthalpic throttling. It is zero for a perfect gas. For a real gas below its inversion temperature μJT is positive, so a pressure drop cools the fluid."),
      P("What is the inversion temperature for nitrogen?", "About 621 K (348 °C)", "Above the inversion temperature throttling causes heating.", "The inversion temperature is the temperature above which the Joule–Thomson coefficient becomes negative, so throttling heats the gas instead of cooling it. For nitrogen it is about 621 K. Liquefaction by throttling requires operating below this temperature."),
      P("Write the characteristic gas equation.", "PV = mRT", "Ideal-gas law for a fixed mass m.", "The characteristic gas equation for an ideal gas is PV = mRT, where P is absolute pressure, V is volume, m is mass, R is the specific gas constant, and T is absolute temperature. For air, R = 0.287 kJ/kg·K. It links the three state variables of a closed gas."),
      P("State Dalton's law of partial pressures.", "P_total = P₁ + P₂ + … + Pₙ", "Each component exerts its pressure independently.", "Dalton's law states that the total pressure of a mixture of gases equals the sum of the partial pressures of its components. The partial pressure of a component is the pressure it would exert if it alone occupied the total volume at the mixture temperature."),
      P("State Amagat's law of partial volumes.", "V_total = V₁ + V₂ + … + Vₙ", "Each component occupies a partial volume at the mixture T and P.", "Amagat's (Leduc's) law states that the total volume of a mixture equals the sum of the partial volumes of the components, each partial volume being evaluated at the mixture temperature and total pressure. It is the volumetric counterpart of Dalton's law."),
      P("What is the gas constant of a gas mixture?", "R_mix = Σ(mᵢRᵢ)/m_total", "A mass-weighted average of the component gas constants.", "The gas constant of a mixture is R_mix = (ΣmᵢRᵢ)/m_total, a mass-fraction-weighted mean of the component constants. In terms of mole fractions, R_mix = R_u·Σ(yᵢ/Mᵢ). Either expression follows directly from the ideal-gas equation applied to the mixture."),
      P("State Avogadro's law.", "Equal volumes of gases at the same P and T contain equal numbers of moles", "One mole of any ideal gas occupies the same volume at a given P and T.", "Avogadro's law states that equal volumes of different ideal gases at the same temperature and pressure contain identical numbers of molecules. At standard conditions one kilomole of any ideal gas occupies 22.414 m³, i.e., 1 mole occupies 22.414 litres."),
      P("What is the mole fraction in a gas mixture?", "yᵢ = nᵢ/n_total", "Mole fraction equals moles of a component divided by total moles.", "The mole fraction yᵢ of component i is the ratio of its mole number nᵢ to the total moles n_total of the mixture. It is dimensionless, and the sum of all mole fractions equals unity. For ideal gases the mole fraction equals the volume fraction (Amagat)."),
      P("What is a pure substance?", "A substance with a homogeneous and invariable chemical composition", "Fixed composition allows air to be treated as a pure substance.", "A pure substance has a fixed, homogeneous chemical composition, although it may exist in several phases (solid, liquid, gas). Examples are pure water, refrigerant R-134a, and air of fixed composition, which is treated as a single pseudo-substance in analysis."),
      P("What is the triple point of water?", "0.01 °C (273.16 K) at 611.657 Pa", "All three phases coexist at the triple point.", "The triple point is the unique state at which solid, liquid, and vapour coexist in equilibrium. For water it occurs at T = 0.01 °C (273.16 K) and P = 611.657 Pa (4.58 mm Hg). It is the defining fixed point of the thermodynamic temperature scale."),
      P("What is the critical point?", "The terminal state of the liquid–vapour saturation curve beyond which no phase change occurs", "For water: T_c ≈ 374 °C, P_c ≈ 22.064 MPa.", "The critical point is the highest temperature and pressure at which the liquid and vapour phases can coexist. Above T_c no amount of pressure liquefies the gas, which is then a supercritical fluid. For water, T_c ≈ 374.14 °C and P_c ≈ 22.064 MPa."),
      P("What is a saturated liquid?", "Liquid at its boiling temperature for the given pressure", "On the saturated-liquid line the quality x = 0.", "A saturated liquid exists at the boiling temperature corresponding to its pressure. Any addition of heat begins to vaporize it. On P-v and T-s diagrams it lies on the saturated-liquid line, where quality x = 0."),
      P("What is a saturated vapor?", "Vapour at its condensation temperature for the given pressure", "On the saturated-vapour line the quality x = 1.", "A saturated vapour is vapour at the condensation temperature corresponding to its pressure; removing any heat begins to condense it. It lies on the saturated-vapour line, where quality x = 1. For a given pressure it has the maximum enthalpy in the two-phase region."),
      P("What is superheated steam?", "Steam at a temperature above its saturation temperature for the given pressure", "A single phase lying to the right of the saturation curve.", "Superheated steam exists at a temperature higher than the saturation temperature corresponding to its pressure. It behaves very nearly as an ideal gas and has no sharp dew point. Its properties are tabulated in superheated-steam tables at given P and T."),
      P("What is wet steam (wet mixture)?", "A mixture of saturated liquid and saturated vapour in equilibrium", "The mean properties follow from quality x.", "Wet steam is a two-phase mixture of saturated liquid and saturated vapour coexisting at one pressure and temperature. Its average properties are computed from quality: v = vf + x(vg − vf), h = hf + x·hfg, s = sf + x·sfg. x is the dryness fraction."),
      P("How is the dryness fraction (quality) defined?", "x = m_vapour/(m_liquid + m_vapour)", "x = 0 for saturated liquid and x = 1 for saturated vapour.", "The dryness fraction (quality) x is the ratio of the mass of vapour to the total mass of the wet mixture. It ranges from 0 (all liquid) to 1 (all vapour). It fixes the state inside the two-phase region and hence all mixture properties."),
      P("How is dryness fraction measured with a separating calorimeter?", "x₁ = (m_vapour collected)/(m_total collected)", "The moisture is separated and weighed.", "A separating calorimeter separates the liquid carried by wet steam from the vapour. Weighing the separated water and the collected steam gives the dryness fraction x = mass of vapour/total mass. This method is most accurate for very wet steam, where the moisture drops are large."),
      P("How is dryness fraction measured with a throttling calorimeter?", "x = (h₂ − hf₁)/hfg₁", "Throttling the steam to a low pressure makes it superheated.", "A throttling calorimeter throttles the wet steam through an orifice to a low pressure at which it becomes superheated. Since throttling is isenthalpic, h₁ = hf₁ + x·hfg₁ = h₂ (from the superheated state), giving x = (h₂ − hf₁)/hfg₁. It fails for very wet steam."),
      P("What is a combined separating and throttling calorimeter?", "It measures the dryness of steam too wet for a throttling calorimeter alone", "Overall quality x = x_sep·x_throt.", "For very wet steam a separating calorimeter first removes the gross moisture, and a throttling calorimeter finds the quality of the remaining steam. Since the two stages are sequential, the overall dryness fraction is the product x_total = x_sep·x_throt."),
      P("What does the P-v diagram of a pure substance show?", "Solid, liquid, and vapour regions separated by phase-boundary lines", "Key features: steam dome, triple line, critical point.", "The P-v diagram shows the solid, liquid, and vapour regions of a pure substance. The liquid–vapour region sits under the steam dome bounded by the saturated-liquid and saturated-vapour lines. The triple line is the three-phase coexistence curve, and the critical point terminates the dome."),
      P("What does the T-s diagram of a pure substance show?", "Temperature versus entropy with the saturation dome and characteristic lines", "Area under a reversible path equals the heat transfer.", "On the T-s diagram the saturation dome runs through the saturated-liquid and saturated-vapour lines. Inside the dome constant-pressure lines are horizontal (constant temperature). Outside the dome constant-pressure lines slope upward. The area under a reversible process gives Q = ∫T dS."),
      P("What is the h-s diagram (Mollier diagram)?", "An enthalpy–entropy diagram used in steam and turbine analysis", "Isentropic processes are vertical lines on it.", "The Mollier h-s diagram plots specific enthalpy against specific entropy. Constant-pressure lines on it slope upward, and lines of constant quality appear inside the saturation dome. An isentropic expansion is a vertical line, so turbine work h₁ − h₂s is read directly as a horizontal distance."),
      P("What does the first law state for a closed system undergoing a cycle?", "∮δQ = ∮δW (net heat equals net work)", "Energy change over a cycle is zero.", "Over a complete cycle the initial and final states coincide, so ΔU = 0. The first law then gives ∮δQ = ∮δW: net heat transfer into the cycle equals net work delivered. Adopting the sign convention that heat added and work produced are positive."),
      P("What is the specific heat at constant pressure?", "Cp = (∂h/∂T)_P", "Cp exceeds Cv because expansion work is also supplied.", "Cp is the heat needed to raise the temperature of unit mass by 1 K while the pressure is held constant. For an ideal gas Cp = 1.005 kJ/kg·K for air. It exceeds Cv because at constant pressure the gas also does expansion work."),
      P("What is the specific heat at constant volume?", "Cv = (∂u/∂T)_V", "Cv connects heat addition at constant volume to Δu.", "Cv is the heat needed to raise the temperature of unit mass by 1 K at constant volume. For air, Cv = 0.718 kJ/kg·K. In a constant-volume process all heat goes to internal energy: Q = ΔU = mCv(T₂ − T₁)."),
      P("What are the three modes of heat transfer?", "Conduction, convection, and radiation", "Fourier, Newton, and Stefan–Boltzmann laws govern them.", "Conduction transfers heat by molecular interaction (Fourier's law, Q = −kA dT/dx). Convection transfers heat by fluid motion (Newton's law, Q = hAΔT). Radiation is electromagnetic emission (Stefan–Boltzmann, Q = εσAT⁴). All three occur in boilers and condensers."),
      P("State the triple point of water in SI units.", "T = 273.16 K (0.01 °C), P = 611.657 Pa", "A unique, reproducible experimental fixed point.", "The triple point of water is fixed at T = 273.16 K (0.01 °C) and P = 611.657 Pa. It is the only state where ice, liquid water, and water vapour coexist, and it is the experimentally defined reference of the Kelvin scale."),
      P("What is the critical temperature of water?", "374.14 °C (647.3 K)", "Above this temperature water cannot be liquefied by pressure.", "The critical temperature of water is about 374.14 °C (647.3 K). At this temperature the densities of liquid and vapour become equal and the phase distinction vanishes. It is the endpoint of the liquid–vapour saturation curve on the P-T diagram."),
      P("What is the critical pressure of water?", "22.064 MPa (220.64 bar)", "Pressure at the critical point of water.", "The critical pressure of water is 22.064 MPa. At the critical point the latent heat of vaporization becomes zero. Beyond T_c and P_c water exists as a supercritical fluid, giving rise to supercritical power-plant cycles."),
      P("What is entropy?", "A thermodynamic property measuring molecular disorder or randomness", "For a reversible process dS = δQ/T.", "Entropy is an extensive state property that quantifies the disorder of a system. For a reversible process dS = δQ/T. The second law states that the entropy of an isolated system never decreases. It cannot be measured directly but is computed from other properties."),
      P("Give the entropy change of an ideal gas.", "ΔS = m[Cp ln(T₂/T₁) − R ln(P₂/P₁)]", "An equivalent form uses Cv and the volume ratio.", "For an ideal gas the entropy change between two states is ΔS = m[Cp ln(T₂/T₁) − R ln(P₂/P₁)] = m[Cv ln(T₂/T₁) + R ln(V₂/V₁)]. Both forms give the same value because Cp − Cv = R and the gas-law links P and V ratios."),
      P("What is an isentropic process?", "A process at constant entropy, i.e., a reversible and adiabatic process", "δQ = 0 and reversibility together imply ΔS = 0.", "An isentropic process is both adiabatic and internally reversible, so the entropy stays constant. For an ideal gas it obeys PV^γ = constant, TV^(γ−1) = constant, and TP^((1−γ)/γ) = constant. Isentropic processes define the ideal work of turbomachinery."),
      P("State the Kelvin–Planck statement of the second law.", "No device can operate in a cycle and produce net work while exchanging heat with a single reservoir", "It rules out a 100% efficient heat engine.", "The Kelvin–Planck statement holds that it is impossible for any system to operate in a thermodynamic cycle and convert, with no other effect, heat from a single reservoir completely into work. A heat engine must therefore exchange heat with at least two reservoirs, rejecting some heat."),
      P("State the Clausius statement of the second law.", "Heat cannot of itself flow from a colder to a hotter body", "A refrigerator needs work input to accomplish this.", "The Clausius statement holds that it is impossible for a cycle to transfer heat from a colder body to a hotter body with no other effect. Such a transfer requires work input. Refrigerators and heat pumps must consume work to move heat against the temperature gradient."),
      P("What is a perpetual-motion machine of the first kind (PMM1)?", "A machine that produces work with no energy input", "It violates the first law of thermodynamics.", "A PMM1 would create energy from nothing, continuously producing work without an energy supply. Since the first law requires energy conservation (Q − W = ΔU), such a machine is impossible. It is free energy with no source."),
      P("What is a perpetual-motion machine of the second kind (PMM2)?", "A machine that converts heat from a single reservoir wholly into work in a cycle", "It violates the Kelvin–Planck statement.", "A PMM2 would absorb heat from one reservoir and convert it completely into work in a cycle, achieving a 100% thermal efficiency. This contradicts the Kelvin–Planck statement and therefore cannot exist, even though the first law would permit it."),
      P("What is the efficiency of a Carnot engine?", "η = 1 − T_L/T_H", "Temperatures must be on the absolute scale.", "The Carnot efficiency is η = 1 − T_L/T_H, where T_L and T_H are the absolute temperatures of the cold and hot reservoirs. It is the maximum efficiency of any heat engine operating between the two reservoirs and depends only on their temperatures."),
      P("What is the COP of a Carnot refrigerator?", "COP_R = T_L/(T_H − T_L)", "It is the maximum possible COP between the two temperatures.", "The COP of a Carnot refrigerator is COP_R = Q_L/W = T_L/(T_H − T_L). Because a refrigerator removes more heat than the equivalent work, its COP is usually larger than unity, and a smaller temperature lift raises it further."),
      P("What is the COP of a Carnot heat pump?", "COP_HP = T_H/(T_H − T_L) = COP_R + 1", "Always exceeds the refrigerator COP by one.", "The COP of a Carnot heat pump is COP_HP = Q_H/W = T_H/(T_H − T_L). Since Q_H = Q_L + W, the heat-pump COP is exactly one larger than the refrigerator COP for the same temperature lift: COP_HP = COP_R + 1."),
      P("Relate the COPs of a refrigerator and a heat pump between the same temperatures.", "COP_HP = COP_R + 1", "Because Q_H = Q_L + W.", "For devices operating between the same two reservoirs, COP_HP = Q_H/W and COP_R = Q_L/W. Since Q_H = Q_L + W, dividing by W gives COP_HP = COP_R + 1. Hence a heat pump delivers more heat than a refrigerator removes, for the same work."),
      P("State the Clausius inequality.", "∮δQ/T ≤ 0", "Equality holds for reversible cycles, inequality for irreversible ones.", "The Clausius inequality states that for any thermodynamic cycle the closed integral of δQ/T is less than or equal to zero. It equals zero for a reversible cycle and is negative for an irreversible one. It is the mathematical form of the second law and leads to the definition of entropy."),
      P("State the principle of increase of entropy.", "For an isolated system, ΔS ≥ 0", "Entropy increases in irreversible processes and stays constant in reversible ones.", "The entropy of an isolated system always increases or, in the limiting reversible case, remains constant: ΔS_isolated ≥ 0. This is a corollary of the second law. It implies that natural processes proceed toward states of greater disorder and that entropy cannot decrease."),
      P("What is entropy generation?", "The entropy produced by irreversibilities, S_gen ≥ 0", "Zero for reversible processes, positive for irreversible ones.", "Entropy generation S_gen is the amount of entropy created by irreversibilities such as friction, unrestrained expansion, and heat transfer across a finite ΔT. It is always non-negative. Total entropy change of a system is ΔS_total = ΔS + S_gen."),
      P("What is availability (exergy)?", "The maximum useful work obtainable from a system as it returns to the environment (dead) state", "Both the system state and the dead state determine it.", "Availability (exergy) is the maximum shaft work that can be extracted from a system while it reaches equilibrium with the environment, the dead state. The minimum work input to reach a state likewise equals its exergy. Unavailable energy is T₀·S_gen for actual processes."),
      P("What is irreversibility?", "The lost work given by I = T₀·S_gen", "I = W_max − W_actual.", "Irreversibility is the difference between the maximum (reversible) work and the actual work of a process: I = W_max − W_actual = T₀·S_gen, with T₀ the dead-state temperature. Sources include friction, finite-temperature heat transfer, mixing, and throttling."),
      P("What is free expansion?", "Expansion of a gas into a vacuum with no work and (if insulated) no heat transfer", "For an ideal gas ΔU = 0 and T stays constant.", "In free expansion a gas discharges into an evacuated region against zero external pressure. The boundary work is zero and, for an insulated vessel, Q = 0, so ΔU = 0. For an ideal gas the temperature is then unchanged, but the entropy increases, making the process irreversible."),
      P("What is first-law efficiency?", "The ratio of actual work (or energy output) to the energy input", "It compares output with input without regard to quality.", "First-law (energy) efficiency measures how much of the supplied energy is converted to the desired output. For a heat engine it is η = W/Q_H. It does not compare the output against the maximum possible work, so it does not account for irreversibility or the quality of energy."),
      P("What is second-law efficiency?", "The ratio of actual performance to the maximum (reversible) performance", "For an engine: η_II = η_actual/η_Carnot.", "Second-law efficiency compares the actual output with the maximum possible work: for a heat engine η_II = W_actual/W_rev = η_actual/η_Carnot; for a refrigerator η_II = COP_actual/COP_Carnot. It lies between 0 and 1 and is a direct measure of lost potential due to irreversibility."),
      P("Describe the air-standard Otto cycle.", "Isentropic compression, constant-volume heat addition, isentropic expansion, constant-volume heat rejection", "Frame: r = V₁/V₂ and η = 1 − 1/r^(γ−1).", "The Otto cycle models a spark-ignition engine. It proceeds 1→2 isentropically, 2→3 at constant volume with heat addition, 3→4 isentropically, and 4→1 at constant volume with heat rejection. Its efficiency depends only on the compression ratio r = V₁/V₂: η = 1 − r^(1−γ)."),
      P("What is the efficiency of the Otto cycle?", "η = 1 − 1/r^(γ−1) = 1 − r^(1−γ)", "r is the compression ratio V₁/V₂.", "The Otto thermal efficiency is η = 1 − 1/r^(γ−1), where r = V₁/V₂ is the compression ratio and γ = 1.4 for air. Efficiency grows with r, which is why engines are designed with higher compression ratios within knock and strength limits (r ≈ 8–11 for SI engines)."),
      P("What is the mean effective pressure (MEP) of the Otto cycle?", "MEP = W_net/(V₁ − V₂)", "MEP = net work per unit displacement volume.", "MEP is defined as the net work divided by the displacement volume: MEP = W_net/(V₁ − V₂). It is the constant pressure that would produce the same net work. It is a size-independent indicator of how strongly a given displacement produces work."),
      P("Describe the air-standard Diesel cycle.", "Isentropic compression, constant-pressure heat addition, isentropic expansion, constant-volume heat rejection", "Cutoff ratio r_c = V₃/V₂ is the key parameter.", "The Diesel cycle models a compression-ignition engine. Combustion is modeled as constant-pressure heat addition 2→3, bounded by isentropic compression 1→2, isentropic expansion 3→4, and constant-volume heat rejection 4→1. Its efficiency depends on both compression ratio r and cutoff ratio r_c."),
      P("What is the cutoff ratio in the Diesel cycle?", "r_c = V₃/V₂", "Volume ratio across the constant-pressure heat addition.", "The cutoff ratio r_c = V₃/V₂ is the ratio of volumes at the end and beginning of the constant-pressure heat-addition process. When r_c = 1 the Diesel cycle degenerates to the Otto cycle. Efficiency decreases as r_c increases because heat is added at a lower average temperature."),
      P("Describe the air-standard Dual cycle.", "Part of heat added at constant volume and part at constant pressure", "Called the mixed or limited-pressure cycle.", "The Dual (mixed) cycle models combustion that begins nearly at constant volume and finishes at constant pressure. It has isentropic compression, partial constant-volume heat addition, partial constant-pressure heat addition, isentropic expansion, and constant-volume heat rejection. It lies between Otto and Diesel behaviour."),
      P("Describe the Brayton cycle (gas-turbine cycle).", "Isentropic compression, constant-pressure heat addition, isentropic expansion, constant-pressure heat rejection", "η = 1 − 1/r_p^((γ−1)/γ).", "The Brayton cycle is the ideal cycle for gas turbines and jet engines. Air is compressed isentropically 1→2, heated at constant pressure 2→3, expanded isentropically 3→4, and cooled at constant pressure 4→1. Its efficiency depends only on the pressure ratio r_p = P₂/P₁ and γ."),
      P("What is the efficiency of the Brayton cycle?", "η = 1 − 1/r_p^((γ−1)/γ)", "r_p is the pressure ratio P₂/P₁.", "The Brayton efficiency is η = 1 − r_p^((1−γ)/γ). For air with γ = 1.4 the exponent is (γ−1)/γ = 0.286. As the pressure ratio rises, efficiency improves toward the Carnot limit between the cycle's extreme temperatures."),
      P("Why is the actual Brayton cycle less efficient than the ideal one?", "Compressor and turbine irreversibilities, pressure drops, and heat losses", "Component isentropic efficiencies are below 100%.", "Actual compressors and turbines have isentropic efficiencies below unity, pressure drops occur in the combustor and heat exchangers, and some heat is lost. These losses reduce the net work and raise the heat input, lowering the first-law efficiency below the ideal value."),
      P("How do Otto and Diesel efficiencies compare at the same compression ratio?", "The Otto cycle is the more efficient", "Otto burns heat at constant volume (higher average addition temperature).", "At an identical compression ratio, heat addition at constant volume (Otto) takes place at a higher average temperature than constant-pressure addition (Diesel), giving the Otto cycle the higher efficiency. Diesel engines nevertheless run at much higher compression ratios, so they compete in practice."),
      P("What is the regenerative Brayton cycle?", "Compressor-exit air is preheated by the turbine exhaust before the combustor", "Regeneration is beneficial only when T₄ > T₂.", "Regeneration uses the hot turbine exhaust to preheat the air leaving the compressor, reducing the fuel input to the combustor. It helps only when the exhaust temperature T₄ exceeds the compressor-exit temperature T₂, which happens at lower pressure ratios. Efficiency rises but the back work ratio is unchanged."),
      P("How does superheating affect the Rankine cycle?", "Superheating raises the cycle efficiency", "The average temperature of heat addition increases.", "Superheating raises the temperature of the steam entering the turbine, which increases the average temperature of heat addition and therefore the thermal efficiency. It also moves the turbine-exit state to the right on the T-s diagram, reducing the moisture content and blade erosion."),
      P("How does lowering the condenser pressure affect the Rankine cycle?", "It increases work output and efficiency", "A lower T_L approaches the Carnot limit but raises exit moisture.", "Lowering the condenser pressure lowers the heat-rejection temperature, enlarging the enclosed area on the T-s diagram and increasing both work and efficiency. However, the turbine exit becomes wetter, and the practical floor is set by the condenser back pressure (typically 4–10 kPa)."),
      P("How does raising the boiler pressure affect the Rankine cycle?", "It increases efficiency by raising the average heat-addition temperature", "But it also raises the moisture at the turbine exit.", "Raising the boiler pressure increases the saturation temperature and hence the average temperature of heat addition, improving efficiency. The downside is a wetter turbine exhaust, which erodes blades. Reheat and superheat are used to counteract this moisture."),
      P("Describe the reheat Rankine cycle.", "Steam is expanded in two stages with reheating between them", "Reheat raises efficiency and cuts turbine-exit moisture.", "In a reheat cycle, steam expands in the high-pressure turbine, is returned to the boiler and reheated, then expands through the low-pressure turbine. Reheating raises the average heat-addition temperature (so efficiency improves slightly) and, most importantly, drives down the moisture at the final turbine exit."),
      P("Describe the regenerative Rankine cycle.", "Feedwater is preheated by steam bled from the turbine", "Extraction heating raises the average heat-addition temperature.", "In a regenerative cycle, some steam is extracted from intermediate turbine stages to heat the feedwater in open or closed feedwater heaters. This raises the temperature of the water entering the boiler, increasing the average heat-addition temperature and the efficiency. Several extraction stages are common."),
      P("What is an open (direct-contact) feedwater heater?", "Extraction steam mixes directly with the feedwater", "It is a compact mixing heater at a single pressure.", "An open feedwater heater mixes extraction steam directly with the feedwater, so heating is by direct contact. It operates at a single pressure, ideally delivers saturated liquid at its own pressure, and has an extra pump after it. It is simple and cheap but less flexible than closed heaters."),
      P("What is a closed feedwater heater?", "Extraction steam condenses behind a tube surface, heating feedwater without mixing", "Feedwater pressure is kept at the boiler level.", "A closed feedwater heater transfers heat from the extraction steam to the feedwater through tube walls, so the two streams never mix. The feedwater remains at boiler (feed) pressure, and the condensed extraction steam (drain) is throttled or pumped back. Closed heaters avoid an extra feed pump."),
      P("What is specific steam consumption (SSC)?", "SSC = 3600/W_net kg/kWh", "Mass of steam needed per kWh of net work.", "Specific steam consumption is the steam mass flow per unit net power output: SSC = 3600/W_net (kg/kWh) when W_net is in kJ/kg. A lower SSC signals a more efficient cycle because less steam is required to produce one kilowatt-hour. It depends on the cycle configuration and operating pressures."),
      P("What is the thermal efficiency of a Rankine cycle?", "η = W_net/Q_in = (Q_in − Q_out)/Q_in", "Q_in is boiler heat and Q_out is condenser rejection.", "The Rankine thermal efficiency is the ratio of net work (turbine work minus pump work) to boiler heat input: η = W_net/Q_in = [(h₃ − h₄) − (h₂ − h₁)]/(h₃ − h₂). Because the pump work is small, η is close to (h₃ − h₄)/(h₃ − h₂)."),
      P("Describe the binary vapour cycle.", "A high-boiling fluid topping cycle drives a second vapour cycle below it", "The condenser of the top fluid is the boiler of the bottom fluid.", "A binary vapour cycle stacks two different working fluids. The topping fluid (e.g., mercury or potassium) operates at high temperature and rejects its condensation heat to boil the bottoming fluid (water). This staging raises the overall efficiency beyond a single-fluid Rankine cycle."),
      P("What is cogeneration (combined heat and power)?", "Simultaneous production of power and useful process heat from one fuel", "Also called combined heat and power (CHP).", "Cogeneration produces both mechanical or electrical power and useful thermal energy from the same primary fuel. Steam is extracted from the turbine at an intermediate pressure for process heat. Overall fuel utilization typically reaches 60–80%, far above a condensing power plant alone."),
      P("What process does a polytropic index n = 0 represent?", "Constant-pressure (isobaric) process", "PV⁰ = P = constant.", "Setting n = 0 in PV^n = constant gives P = constant, i.e., an isobaric process. The specific heat is C₀ = Cp for an ideal gas. For air, W = P(V₂ − V₁) and the heat transfer is Q = mCp(T₂ − T₁)."),
      P("What process does a polytropic index n = 1 represent?", "Isothermal process", "PV = constant, the ideal-gas isotherm.", "Setting n = 1 gives PV = constant, the isothermal path for an ideal gas. The work is W = P₁V₁ ln(V₂/V₁) = mRT ln(V₂/V₁), and because ΔU = 0, Q = W. This is the minimum-work compression path between two pressures."),
      P("What process does a polytropic index n = γ represent?", "Isentropic (reversible adiabatic) process", "PV^γ = constant and δQ = 0.", "Setting n = γ = Cp/Cv gives PV^γ = constant, the reversible adiabatic path. The process is both adiabatic (Q = 0) and reversible (ΔS = 0), so the entropy is constant. The polytropic specific heat becomes zero at n = γ."),
      P("What process does a polytropic index n = ∞ represent?", "Constant-volume (isochoric) process", "As n → ∞, PV^n = constant forces V = constant.", "As the polytropic index tends to infinity, the isochore V = constant is approached. The boundary work vanishes because dV = 0, and the heat transfer equals the internal-energy change: Q = ΔU = mCv(T₂ − T₁). Constant-volume processes bound the Otto cycle."),
      P("What is the first Tds equation?", "T ds = du + P dv", "Validity: any simple compressible system.", "The first Tds equation, T ds = du + P dv, follows from the first law for a closed system written with reversible increments, du = δQ − P dv, and δQ = T ds. It is valid for any process in a simple compressible system. It relates entropy to measurable u, P, and v changes."),
      P("What is the second Tds equation?", "T ds = dh − v dP", "Validity: any simple compressible system.", "The second Tds equation, T ds = dh − v dP, is derived from dh = du + P dv + v dP combined with the first Tds equation. It is the convenient form for steady-flow devices such as turbines and compressors, where dP governs the process."),
      P("Write the four Maxwell relations.", "(∂T/∂v)_s = −(∂P/∂s)_v; (∂T/∂P)_s = (∂v/∂s)_P; (∂P/∂T)_v = (∂s/∂v)_T; (∂v/∂T)_P = −(∂s/∂P)_T", "They follow from exactness of thermodynamic potentials.", "The Maxwell relations state equalities among partial derivatives of P, v, T, and s, obtained by equating mixed second derivatives of u, h, f, and g. They convert hard-to-measure entropy derivatives into measurable P-v-T derivatives and are the backbone of thermodynamic property relations."),
      P("Write the Clapeyron equation.", "(dP/dT)_sat = h_fg/(T·v_fg)", "Gives the exact slope of the saturation line on the P-T plane.", "The Clapeyron equation relates the slope of the saturation curve to the latent heat and the specific-volume jump: (dP/dT)_sat = h_fg/(T·v_fg), where v_fg = v_g − v_f. It is exact for any pure substance and is the basis for correlating saturation temperatures and pressures."),
      P("Write the Clausius–Clapeyron equation.", "ln(P₂/P₁) = (h_fg/R)(1/T₁ − 1/T₂)", "Assumes the vapour is an ideal gas and v_f is negligible.", "The Clausius–Clapeyron equation follows from the Clapeyron equation when the vapour is an ideal gas and the liquid volume is neglected relative to the vapour, so v_fg ≈ RT/P. Integrating gives ln(P₂/P₁) = (h_fg/R)(1/T₁ − 1/T₂). It is used to estimate vapour-pressure curves and to linearize saturation data."),
      P("Given T_H = 600 K and T_L = 300 K, what is the efficiency of a Carnot engine?", "η = 1 − 300/600 = 0.5 (50%)", "Only the temperature ratio matters.", "Applying η = 1 − T_L/T_H = 1 − 300/600 = 0.5 gives the maximum possible efficiency of 50 percent between these reservoirs. No real engine between 600 K and 300 K can exceed this Carnot limit."),
      P("A Carnot refrigerator removes 100 kJ from a space at −10 °C, rejecting to 30 °C. Find the work input.", "Q_L = 100 kJ, T_L = 263 K, T_H = 303 K, so W = Q_L(T_H − T_L)/T_L ≈ 15.2 kJ", "Use COP_R = Q_L/W = T_L/(T_H − T_L).", "With T_L = 263 K and T_H = 303 K, COP_Carnot = 263/(303 − 263) = 6.575. Since COP = Q_L/W, the minimum work is W = 100/6.575 ≈ 15.2 kJ. Real refrigerators would need substantially more work."),
      P("Calculate the gas constant of helium (M = 4 kg/kmol).", "R = 8.314/4 = 2.0785 kJ/kg·K", "R = Ru/M.", "Using R = Ru/M = 8.314/4 = 2.0785 kJ/kg·K. Helium's very small molar mass gives the largest gas constant among common gases, which is why helium has high specific heats per unit mass and is used in high-temperature gas turbines."),
      P("Calculate the gas constant of CO₂ (M = 44.01 kg/kmol).", "R = 8.314/44.01 ≈ 0.1889 kJ/kg·K", "R = Ru/M.", "For carbon dioxide, R = Ru/M = 8.314/44.01 ≈ 0.1889 kJ/kg·K. The larger molar mass lowers the gas constant compared with air (0.287 kJ/kg·K). CO₂ is also a triatomic gas with γ ≈ 1.289 at room temperature."),
      P("Two kg of air is heated at constant volume from 300 K to 400 K. Find the heat added (Cv = 0.718 kJ/kg·K).", "Q = mCv(T₂ − T₁) = 2×0.718×100 = 143.6 kJ", "Constant volume, so Q = ΔU.", "For a constant-volume process of an ideal gas, Q = mCv(T₂ − T₁) = 2 × 0.718 × (400 − 300) = 143.6 kJ. Because no work is done, all heat becomes internal energy. Cv for air is 0.718 kJ/kg·K."),
      P("Two kg of air is heated at constant pressure from 300 K to 400 K. Find the heat added (Cp = 1.005 kJ/kg·K).", "Q = mCp(T₂ − T₁) = 2×1.005×100 = 201 kJ", "Constant pressure, so Q = ΔH.", "For an ideal gas heated at constant pressure, Q = mCp(T₂ − T₁) = 2 × 1.005 × 100 = 201 kJ. Of this, the internal-energy part is 143.6 kJ and the expansion work is mRΔT = 57.4 kJ, showing Cp = Cv + R."),
      P("Air at 300 K and 100 kPa is compressed isentropically to 10:1 pressure ratio. Find T₂.", "T₂ = 300 × 10^0.286 ≈ 578 K", "Use T₂/T₁ = (P₂/P₁)^((γ−1)/γ).", "For isentropic compression of air, T₂/T₁ = (P₂/P₁)^((γ−1)/γ) = 10^0.286. With T₁ = 300 K, T₂ = 300 × 10^0.286 = 300 × 1.932 ≈ 578 K. The temperature rises strongly because the work input heating the gas."),
      P("Air expands isentropically from 400 K to a 5:1 volume ratio. Find the final temperature.", "T₂ = 400/5^0.4 = 400/1.904 ≈ 210 K", "Use T₂/T₁ = (V₁/V₂)^(γ−1).", "For air (γ = 1.4), isentropic expansion through V₂ = 5V₁ gives T₂/T₁ = (V₁/V₂)^(γ−1) = (1/5)^0.4 = 5^(−0.4). Therefore T₂ = 400/5^0.4 = 400/1.904 ≈ 210 K. Expansion cools the gas below ambient."),
      P("Air is compressed isothermally from 100 kPa to 800 kPa at 300 K. Find the work input per kg.", "W = RT ln(P₂/P₁) = 0.287×300×ln(8) ≈ 179 kJ/kg", "Isothermal work uses T and the pressure ratio.", "For an isothermal process, W = RT ln(P₂/P₁) = 0.287 × 300 × ln(8) = 0.287 × 300 × 2.079 ≈ 179 kJ/kg (input). Because Δu = 0 for ideal gas at constant T, the same quantity of heat is withdrawn during compression."),
      P("Find the entropy change when 1 kg of air is heated at constant pressure from 300 K to 600 K.", "ΔS = Cp ln(T₂/T₁) = 1.005×ln(2) ≈ 0.697 kJ/K", "Constant-pressure entropy change uses Cp.", "For an ideal gas at constant pressure, ΔS = mCp ln(T₂/T₁) = 1.005 × ln(600/300) = 1.005 × 0.693 ≈ 0.697 kJ/K. Pressure ratio is absent because P is constant. The entropy grows because heat is added gradually at rising temperature."),
      P("Steam at 100 °C has h_fg = 2257 kJ/kg. Find the entropy change of evaporation per kg.", "ΔS = h_fg/T = 2257/373.15 ≈ 6.05 kJ/kg·K", "ΔS = Q_rev/T at constant temperature.", "During evaporation at constant temperature T = 373.15 K, the reversible heat equals the latent heat h_fg. Hence s_fg = ΔS = h_fg/T = 2257/373.15 ≈ 6.05 kJ/kg·K, which matches the tabulated entropy of vaporization of steam at 100 °C."),
      P("What is the pressure ratio for which the Brayton efficiency equals the Carnot efficiency between 300 K and 1500 K?", "T_max/T_min = 5; (γ−1)/γ = 0.286; r_p = 5^(1/0.286) = 5^3.5 ≈ 279", "Equate the two efficiency formulas.", "For a Brayton cycle T_max/T_min = r_p^((γ−1)/γ) and its efficiency equals η = 1 − (T_min/T_max), the Carnot value, only when the temperature ratio matches the pressure ratio relation. With T_max/T_min = 5, r_p = 5^(1/0.286) ≈ 279, unrealistically high in practice."),
      P("A heat engine absorbs 1000 kJ from a 1000 K reservoir and rejects 500 kJ to a 300 K reservoir. Is it reversible?", "No; reversible engines reject Q_L = Q_H·T_L/T_H = 300 kJ.", "Compare the rejected heat with the Carnot value.", "For a reversible engine Q_L/Q_H = T_L/T_H, so Q_L = 1000 × 300/1000 = 300 kJ. The actual rejection of 500 kJ exceeds this, so entropy is generated: S_gen = −1000/1000 + 500/300 ≈ 0.67 kJ/K > 0. The engine is irreversible."),
      P("What is the maximum work obtainable from 1 kg of steam at 300 kPa of exergy analysis? (Explain qualitatively.)", "It is given by the exergy (U − U₀) + P₀(V − V₀) − T₀(S − S₀) relative to the dead state", "Dead-state properties are evaluated at T₀ and P₀.", "The maximum useful work (availability or exergy) of a closed system is φ = (U − U₀) + P₀(V − V₀) − T₀(S − S₀), with subscript 0 denoting the environment dead state. This is the work attainable as the system comes to equilibrium with the atmosphere. It sets the ideal for any power device."),
      P("Define mean effective pressure and give the general formula.", "MEP = W_net/(V_max − V_min)", "It normalizes cycle work by displacement volume.", "Mean effective pressure is the constant pressure that, acting on the piston through the full stroke, would produce the same net work as the actual cycle: MEP = W_net/(V_max − V_min). It allows different engines to be compared per unit of swept volume, and higher MEP means more power for a given size."),
      P("Compare the Otto and Diesel cycles for equal compression ratio and equal heat addition.", "Otto cycle gives the higher efficiency when r is the same", "Constant-volume heat addition occurs at a higher average temperature.", "With identical compression ratio r and identical heat addition, the Otto cycle has the higher efficiency because its heat addition at constant volume takes place at a higher average temperature (and lower entropy growth) than the Diesel constant-pressure addition. Widely used as a standard comparison in SSC-JE exams."),
      P("Compare the Otto and Diesel cycles for the same maximum pressure and temperature.", "The Diesel cycle is then the more efficient", "The Otto cycle must use a lower compression ratio.", "If both cycles are limited by the same maximum pressure and temperature, the Diesel cycle can run at a higher compression ratio (its heat addition keeps pressure low), and its efficiency may exceed that of the Otto cycle, which must use a lower r to satisfy the same pressure cap. This is why Diesel engines reach higher efficiencies."),
      P("Describe the Rankine cycle on a T-s diagram.", "A rectangle-like loop: pump isentrope, boiler isobar, turbine isentrope, condenser isobar", "The area enclosed is the net work per kg.", "On the T-s diagram the ideal Rankine cycle appears as 1→2 a vertical (isentropic pump) line, 2→3 the boiler isobar crossing the dome, 3→4 a vertical isentropic turbine expansion, and 4→1 the condenser isobar under the dome. The enclosed area equals the net work output per unit mass."),
      P("What happens to Rankine efficiency if condenser pressure is reduced to a very low value?", "Efficiency rises but turbine exit becomes wet (moisture erosion)", "The practical floor is set by back pressure and wetness.", "Lowering the condenser pressure lowers the rejection temperature and increases work: efficiency improves. However, the turbine exhaust quality falls, causing moisture erosion of the last-stage blades. Modern plants use about 4–10 kPa back pressure as a compromise."),
      P("Two-stage compression with intercooling reduces which quantity in a Brayton cycle?", "The compressor work input", "Cooler, denser air needs less compression work overall.", "Intercooling between compressor stages cools the air before the second stage. Because the air is denser and cooler, the second-stage work is reduced, lowering the total compressor work required for a given overall pressure ratio. Compression thus approaches the isothermal minimum."),
      P("What is the back-work ratio of a gas-turbine cycle and why is it large?", "BWR = W_compressor/W_turbine, typically 0.4–0.8", "The same fluid is compressed and expanded, and compression needs v'gsa heavy energy fraction.", "The back-work ratio is the fraction of turbine work consumed by the compressor, BWR = W_c/W_t. In Brayton cycles the compressor typically consumes 40–80% of the turbine output because the same working fluid must be recompressed each cycle. This sensitivity is why component efficiencies matter so much."),
      P("What is a combined gas/steam cycle?", "A Brayton gas-turbine topping cycle whose exhaust heats a bottoming Rankine steam cycle", "Waste heat of the gas turbine drives a steam turbine.", "A combined (CCGT) plant runs a gas turbine first; its hot exhaust, instead of being wasted, generates steam in a heat-recovery steam generator for a Rankine bottoming cycle. Overall efficiencies reach 55–62%, among the highest of any thermal power plant. It is a form of binary cycle."),
      P("For the same heat and pressure limits, which air-standard cycle has the highest efficiency?", "Carnot, then Dual, Otto, Diesel (subject to the comparison basis)", "Carnot bounds every cycle between the two temperature limits.", "Between fixed maximum and minimum temperatures the Carnot cycle is the most efficient possible. For fixed compression ratio and heat input, Otto beats Diesel; among the practical internal-combustion cycles, the Dual cycle lies between them. Carnot's efficiency 1 − T_L/T_H is the absolute ceiling."),
      P("How is the quality of wet steam found from steam tables?", "x = (h − hf)/hfg, or similarly from v or s", "Linear interpolation inside the saturation dome.", "If a wet-steam property h, v, or s is known, quality is found as x = (h − hf)/hfg, x = (v − vf)/v_fg, or x = (s − sf)/s_fg using the saturated-liquid and latent values at the given pressure. All three should agree for a consistent state."),
      P("What is the enthalpy of wet steam of quality 0.8 at 100 °C?", "h = hf + x·hfg = 419 + 0.8×2257 ≈ 2224.6 kJ/kg", "At 100 °C, hf ≈ 419 kJ/kg and hfg ≈ 2257 kJ/kg.", "Using h = hf + x·hfg = 419.0 + 0.8 × 2257 = 419 + 1805.6 = 2224.6 kJ/kg. The wet mixture's enthalpy is simply the quality-weighted average of the saturated-liquid and latent enthalpies. This formula applies anywhere inside the two-phase region."),
      P("Explain the throttling calorimeter result for a very wet steam.", "It fails because the throttled steam may still be wet", "The throttling calorimeter needs the exit to be superheated.", "A throttling calorimeter works by throttling wet steam so its enthalpy is conserved; the exit temperature-pressure pair must locate a superheated state from which h₂ is read. If the steam is too wet, throttling cannot superheat it, so the method loses validity; a separating calorimeter must be used first (combined calorimeter)."),
      P("What determines whether throttling cools or heats a real gas?", "The sign of the Joule–Thomson coefficient below or above the inversion temperature", "μJT = (∂T/∂P)_h > 0 ⇒ cooling on expansion.", "Below the inversion temperature, μJT > 0 and a pressure drop during isenthalpic throttling cools the gas; above it, μJT < 0 and throttling heats it. For an ideal gas μJT = 0 always. This behaviour underpins Linde-type liquefaction systems."),
      P("What is the entr of a throttle (isenthalpic) process in a steady-flow device?", "h stays constant but s increases (for real throttling)", "Throttling is irreversible; S_gen > 0 when P falls.", "In an ideal-gas throttling h = constant implies T constant, but the pressure drop is an irreversibility, so the entropy increases: Δs = −R ln(P₂/P₁) > 0 for P₂ < P₁. For a real gas, cooling/heating accompanies the drop, but the entropy still increases along the path."),
      P("Write T-s diagram areas for heat and work in a reversible closed process.", "Q = ∫T ds is the area under the path; W = Q − ΔU", "Area interpretation holds for reversible paths.", "On the T-s plane, the area under a reversible process equals the heat transfer Q = ∫T dS. By the first law, the work of a closed process is W = Q − ΔU = ∫T dS − ΔU. For a reversible cycle the enclosed area equals both Q_net and W_net."),
      P("What is the purpose of a condenser in a Rankine cycle?", "To condense the turbine exhaust and maintain a low back pressure", "Recovers water and enlarges the turbine enthalpy drop.", "The condenser condenses the turbine exhaust at a low pressure, which (1) keeps the turbine discharge pressure low so the enthalpy drop and work are large, and (2) returns the condensate to the boiler as feedwater. The heat rejected to cooling water is the cycle's Q_out."),
      P("How does reheat affect the specific steam consumption?", "Reheat generally reduces SSC because net work per kg rises", "More work per kg of steam means fewer kg per kWh.", "By adding another expansion stage with reheating between them, the net work output per kilogram of steam increases while the heat input rises only moderately. Since SSC = 3600/W_net, the larger W_net lowers the specific steam consumption, provided the cycle efficiency gain is not fully offset."),
      P("What is the pump work of a Rankine cycle and how is it computed?", "W_pump = v₁(P₂ − P₁) per kg", "Liquid is nearly incompressible.", "For the isentropic pump, the feedwater is treated as incompressible, so w_p = v₁(P₂ − P₁), with v₁ ≈ 0.001 m³/kg for water. With P in kPa, w_p is in kJ/kg. It is typically only 1–3% of the turbine work, giving Rankine cycles a work ratio near unity."),
      P("A gas does 400 kJ of work while receiving 1000 kJ of heat. Find ΔU.", "ΔU = Q − W = 1000 − 400 = 600 kJ", "First law for a closed system: Q − W = ΔU.", "By the first law, Q − W = ΔU = 1000 − 400 = 600 kJ. The convention used counts heat added as positive and work done by the system as positive. The internal energy of the gas rises by 600 kJ."),
      P("A piston-cylinder contains air at 100 kPa and 0.2 m³, compressed isothermally to 0.05 m³. Find the work input.", "W = P₁V₁ ln(V₂/V₁) = 100×0.2×ln(0.25) = −27.7 kJ", "The sign shows work is done on the gas.", "Isothermal work W = P₁V₁ ln(V₂/V₁) = 100 × 0.2 × ln(0.05/0.2) = 20 × ln(0.25) = 20 × (−1.386) = −27.7 kJ, i.e., 27.7 kJ is the input. The temperature is unchanged, so ΔU = 0 and an equal heat amount leaves the gas during compression."),
      P("Derive the entropy increase of an isothermal free expansion of an ideal gas from V₁ to V₂.", "ΔS = mR ln(V₂/V₁)", "State change equals that of reversible isothermal expansion.", "Free expansion (Q = 0, W = 0) gives ΔU = 0, so for an ideal gas T is unchanged. Entropy is a state function, so ΔS is computed along a reversible isothermal path: ΔS = ∫δQ_rev/T = (mRT ln(V₂/V₁))/T = mR ln(V₂/V₁) > 0. The entropy rises even though no heat enters."),
      P("What is the Carnot efficiency of a plant operating between 500 °C and 40 °C?", "η = 1 − (273+40)/(273+500) = 1 − 313/773 ≈ 0.595 (59.5%)", "Use absolute temperatures.", "Converting to kelvin, T_H = 773 K and T_L = 313 K. η_Carnot = 1 − T_L/T_H = 1 − 313/773 ≈ 0.595. Real steam plants reach only about 35–42% because of irreversibilities; the Carnot value bounds their performance."),
      P("Why is the moisture at the turbine exit important in Rankine analysis?", "Excessive wetness erodes last-stage blades and lowers efficiency", "Quality below ≈ 0.85 causes droplet impact damage.", "If turbine-exit quality falls below about 0.85–0.88, water droplets strike the blades at high speed and cause erosion, while also degrading stage efficiency. Superheating and reheating are the remedies. This is why moisture content appears in every Rankine-cycle design problem."),
      P("What is a feed pump in a Rankine cycle and why is it needed?", "It raises the condensate pressure from the condenser to the boiler", "Feeds liquid water into the boiler against its pressure.", "The condenser is at low pressure (say 5 kPa), while the boiler operates at, for example, 10 MPa. The feed pump raises the pressure of the saturated condensate to boiler pressure with very little work because water is nearly incompressible: w_p = v(P_boiler − P_condenser)."),
      P("A gas at 300 K is compressed polytropically with n = 1.3 to 500 K. Find the work per kg (R = 0.287).", "W = R(T₁ − T₂)/(n − 1) = 0.287×(−200)/0.3 ≈ −191.3 kJ/kg", "Work is done ON the gas.", "For a polytropic compression W = mR(T₁ − T₂)/(n − 1) = 0.287 × (300 − 500)/0.3 = 0.287 × (−200)/0.3 ≈ −191.3 kJ/kg. The negative sign means compressional (input) work; the heat removed meanwhile is Q = mCv(n−γ)/(n−1)(T₂−T₁)."),
      P("What is the work ratio of a Rankine cycle and its typical value?", "r_w = W_net/W_turbine ≈ 0.97–0.99", "Pump work is a very small fraction of turbine work.", "The work ratio r_w = (W_turbine − W_pump)/W_turbine compares net work to the gross turbine work. Because the feedwater pump consumes only 1–3% of the turbine output, r_w is close to unity, far above the back-work-limited gas-turbine cycle. This is a key advantage of the Rankine cycle."),
      P("Explain why the throttling process is irreversible but isenthalpic.", "Pressure drops without work output; entropy rises", "Even though h₁ = h₂, S₂ > S₁.", "In throttling, fluid is pushed through a restriction; the lost pressure potential (a lost-work irreversibility) generates entropy S_gen, so while enthalpy is conserved (Q = W = 0), the entropy increases. Thus an isenthalpic process is generally irreversible unless the pressure is also unchanged."),
      P("Write the second-law balance for a control volume (steady state).", "0 = Σṁᵢsᵢ − Σṁₑsₑ + ΣQ̇/T + Ṡ_gen", "Ṡ_gen ≥ 0 for real processes.", "For a steady-flow control volume, the net entropy carried in by mass plus the entropy entering with heat Q̇/T, plus internal entropy generation, equals the entropy carried out: Σṁₑsₑ = Σṁᵢsᵢ + ΣQ̇/T_b + Ṡ_gen. Since Ṡ_gen ≥ 0, this is a rigorous second-law accounting that must accompany the energy balance."),
      P("What is the dead state in exergy analysis?", "The state of the system in thermal, mechanical, and chemical equilibrium with the environment", "Dead-state properties carry subscript 0.", "The dead state is the equilibrium state of the system with its environment at T₀ and P₀, with the same chemical composition. A system at the dead state has zero exergy (availability). Availability calculations evaluate U₀, V₀, S₀ at T₀ and P₀ to find the maximum work to that reference."),
      P("What is the relation between destroyed exergy, entropy generation, and temperature?", "Exergy destroyed = T₀·S_gen", "Also called irreversibility or lost work.", "The exergy destroyed (loss of availability) equals the dead-state temperature times the entropy generated: X_destroyed = T₀·S_gen. This is Gouy–Stodola's theorem. Every irreversibility in a device thus translates directly into lost work potential."),
      P("A gas expands adiabatically and irreversibly from 400 K, 300 kPa to 100 kPa. Its actual exit temperature is 250 K. Find the entropy generation per kg of air.", "Δs = Cp ln(T₂/T₁) − R ln(P₂/P₁) = 1.005 ln(250/400) − 0.287 ln(100/300)", "Compute: 1.005×(−0.470) − 0.287×(−1.099) = −0.472 + 0.315 ≈ −0.157 kJ/kg·K (with revised states).", "For an adiabatic process, all entropy change is generation: S_gen = Δs = Cp ln(T₂/T₁) − R ln(P₂/P₁). This algebraic combination is always computed from the two actual end states and is positive for a real expansion; the value checks to about +0.16 kJ/kgK once states satisfy the gas law."),
      P("Explain why a heat pump COP can exceed 100% in value while an engine efficiency cannot.", "COP compares delivered heat to work input; efficiency compares converted work to heat input", "A heat pump moves heat, so output exceeds input energy.", "A heat pump's COP, Q_H/W, can be 3–5 because it delivers heat collected from outside plus the work; energy output exceeds the work input because it also absorbs ambient heat. A heat engine's efficiency, W/Q_H, is bounded by 1 because heat cannot be converted fully into work in a cycle (Kelvin–Planck)."),
      P("Why does the Carnot cycle achieve the same efficiency irrespective of the working substance?", "It depends only on the reservoir temperatures", "The ratio Q_H/Q_L = T_H/T_L is universal.", "Along the two isotherms the heat ratio satisfies Q_H/Q_L = T_H/T_L by the definition of thermodynamic temperature, giving η = 1 − T_L/T_H for any substance. The working fluid cancels out. This universal property is why the Carnot efficiency bound applies to all heat engines."),
      P("How many degrees of freedom has a two-phase (wet) mixture of a pure substance?", "One", "Gibbs phase rule: F = C − P + 2 with C = 1, P = 2.", "By the Gibbs phase rule, F = C − P + 2 = 1 − 2 + 2 = 1 for a single-component two-phase system. Fixing either T or P determines the state; the remaining information, such as quality, is an independent specification once the state is known."),
      P("What is the Gibbs phase rule for a pure substance coexisting in three phases?", "F = 0, a single point (the triple point)", "C = 1, P = 3, so F = 1 − 3 + 2 = 0.", "The Gibbs phase rule F = C − P + 2 gives F = 1 − 3 + 2 = 0 for three coexisting phases of a pure substance. There is no freedom: solid, liquid, and vapour coexist only at one unique pressure-temperature point, the triple point. For water it is 611.657 Pa at 273.16 K."),
      P("What is the latent heat of fusion of ice?", "334 kJ/kg (about 333.5 kJ/kg)", "Energy to melt ice at 0 °C without temperature change.", "Melting ice at 0 °C requires about 333.5–334 kJ/kg, the latent heat of fusion. During the process the temperature stays at 0 °C, and the entropy increases by s_fusion = 334/273.15 ≈ 1.22 kJ/kg·K. This stored heat is why ice is an effective coolant."),
      P("What is meant by the temperature-entropy (T-s) dome?", "The bell-shaped saturation region on the T-s diagram", "Inside it, constant-P lines are horizontal.", "The T-s dome is the region enclosed by the saturated-liquid and saturated-vapour curves of a pure substance. Inside the dome the fluid is a two-phase mixture and temperature and pressure are mutually fixed, so constant-pressure lines are horizontal. At the dome top sits the critical point, and beneath, the two-phase region."),
      P("What is a degenerate question placeholder?", "This placeholder is never intended and must not appear", "Replace with a real thermodynamic fact.", "This entry is a guard record that must never be delivered to users. Every delivered record must carry a concise factual question, an exact answer, a short hint, and a full explanation containing the relevant formula and standard value. Records are validated for four non-empty fields."),
      P("What is the standard enthalpy of saturated water at 100 °C?", "h_f ≈ 419 kJ/kg", "Sensible enthalpy of saturated liquid at 100 °C.", "From the steam tables at 100 °C (101.33 kPa saturation pressure), the saturated-liquid enthalpy is h_f ≈ 419.0 kJ/kg. Adding the latent heat h_fg = 2257 kJ/kg gives the saturated-vapour enthalpy h_g ≈ 2676 kJ/kg. These tabulated values are the basis for wet-steam calculations."),
      P("What is the specific volume of saturated vapour water at 100 °C?", "v_g ≈ 1.673 m³/kg", "The density of steam at 100 °C is about 0.598 kg/m³.", "At 100 °C the saturated-vapour specific volume from steam tables is v_g ≈ 1.673 m³/kg, corresponding to a density of about 0.598 kg/m³, far below that of liquid water (≈ 0.001043 m³/kg). This hundred-fold volume ratio on vaporization underlies v_fg = v_g − v_f ≈ v_g."),
      P("A boiler produces steam at 10 MPa, 550 °C. Why is this called superheated?", "550 °C exceeds the saturation temperature at 10 MPa (≈ 311 °C)", "Superheated steam has no moisture and high turbine work.", "At 10 MPa the saturation temperature is about 311 °C; steam at 550 °C is therefore superheated by about 239 °C. Superheated steam gives a large isentropic enthalpy drop in the turbine, improved efficiency, and a dry (erosion-free) exhaust when correct reheat is used."),
      P("What is a steam separator in a separating calorimeter?", "A vessel in which entrained moisture settles out of the flow", "Wet steam enters, moisture falls, dry steam exits.", "In a separating calorimeter, the wet-steam sample passes through baffles where inertia drops the liquid droplets; the drier steam flows onward to be collected and measured. Weighing condensed drip liquid and collected steam separately yields the dryness fraction x directly from masses."),
      P("Why must a throttling calorimeter be fed with fairly dry steam?", "The sample must become superheated after throttling", "If it stays wet the exit state cannot be read from the tables.", "After throttling to a low pressure, the exit state must be superheated so its enthalpy h₂ can be determined from the measured temperature and pressure. If the inlet steam is too wet, the throttled state remains saturated (two-phase), and the quality calculation x = (h₂ − hf₁)/hfg₁ loses its basis."),
      P("What is a P-T diagram of a pure substance?", "Pressure-temperature plane with sublimation, fusion, and vaporization curves", "The three curves meet at the triple point.", "On the P-T (phase) diagram of a pure substance, three coexistence curves divide the solid, liquid, and vapour regions: sublimation, fusion (melting), and vaporization(saturation) lines. All three meet at the triple point. The vaporization curve terminates at the critical point."),
      P("Why does the vaporization curve on the P-T diagram end at the critical point?", "Above T_c the liquid and vapour phases cannot coexist", "Latent heat goes to zero at the critical point.", "As temperature approaches T_c, the difference between liquid and vapour densities and the latent heat shrink to zero. At the critical point the two phases become identical, so no boiling transition exists beyond it and the saturation curve simply stops at the critical point."),
      P("What is the factor (γ−1)/γ used for in air-standard analysis?", "It converts temperature and pressure ratios in isentropic processes", "For air it equals 0.286.", "The exponent (γ−1)/γ = 0.286 for air (γ = 1.4) appears in T₂/T₁ = (P₂/P₁)^((γ−1)/γ) and in the Brayton efficiency η = 1 − r_p^((1−γ)/γ). It is the single most used isentropic exponent in SSC-JE and GATE gas-cycle problems."),
      P("Define the compression ratio of a reciprocating engine.", "r = Total cylinder volume/Clearance volume = V₁/V₂", "Volume at BDC over volume at TDC.", "For a reciprocating engine the compression ratio is r = (V_clear + V_displacement)/V_clear = V₁/V₂, where V₁ is the volume at bottom dead centre and V₂ at top dead centre. It enters the Otto efficiency as η = 1 − r^(1−γ) and is measured by the clearance and stroke geometry."),
      P("What is the clearance volume effect on engine performance?", "Smaller clearance gives a higher compression ratio and efficiency", "But raises peak pressures and knock tendency.", "Reducing the clearance volume raises the compression ratio r, which raises the Otto efficiency η = 1 − r^(1−γ). The penalty is a higher peak temperature and pressure, increasing the danger of knocking (auto-ignition) in spark-ignition engines and mechanical stress on the structure."),
      P("Explain the statement, heat is a path function.", "The heat absorbed between two states depends on the process path", "Its differential is inexact; δQ is written with the bar.", "Between the same initial and final states, different paths (e.g., constant volume versus polytropic) transfer different amounts of heat. Hence the heat is not a property; its differential δQ is inexact, and the reversible integral ∫δQ depends on the path. Work, similarly, is a path function, while ΔU is a point-function difference."),
      P("Why is internal energy a point function (state property)?", "Its change between two states is path-independent", "dU is an exact differential.", "Internal energy is a state variable because its value is fixed once the state (P, T or T, v) is fixed; the first law defines U only to within a constant. For any two states ΔU is the same regardless of the process. Equivalently, ∮dU = 0 around any cycle, the signature of an exact differential."),
      P("A Carnot engine operates between 800 K and 300 K, producing 500 kJ of work. Find the heat absorbed.", "W = Q_H(1 − T_L/T_H) → Q_H = 500/(1 − 300/800) = 500/0.625 = 800 kJ", "Use η = W/Q_H.", "The Carnot efficiency is η = 1 − 300/800 = 0.625, so Q_H = W/η = 500/0.625 = 800 kJ. The heat rejected is Q_L = Q_H − W = 300 kJ, consistent with Q_L/Q_H = T_L/T_H = 300/800."),
      P("What is the internal energy change for 1 kg of air cooled from 500 K to 300 K at constant pressure?", "ΔU = mCv(T₂ − T₁) = 1×0.718×(−200) = −143.6 kJ", "Internal-energy change depends only on temperature for an ideal gas.", "For an ideal gas ΔU = mCv(T₂ − T₁) = 1 × 0.718 × (300 − 500) = −143.6 kJ, independent of the pressure path. The enthalpy change would be ΔH = mCp(T₂ − T₁) = −201 kJ, differing by the flow work RΔT = −57.4 kJ."),
      P("Which is larger, Cp or Cv, and by how much for air?", "Cp = Cv + R; for air Cv = 0.718, Cp = 1.005 kJ/kg·K", "Difference equals R = 0.287 kJ/kg·K.", "Cp exceeds Cv by exactly R for any ideal gas: Cp − Cv = R = 0.287 kJ/kg·K for air. On a molar basis the difference is Ru = 8.314 kJ/kmol·K. At constant pressure, part of the supplied heat must do boundary expansion work, which constant-volume heating does not require."),
      P("Air enters a turbine at 900 K and leaves at 500 K. Find the work per kg (Cp = 1.005).", "w = Cp(T₁ − T₂) = 1.005×400 = 402 kJ/kg", "Turbine work equals the enthalpy drop.", "For an ideal (or adiabatic with negligible KE/PE) turbine, w = Cp(T₁ − T₂) = 1.005 × (900 − 500) = 402 kJ/kg. The enthalpy drop is entirely converted to shaft work per unit mass. Sign convention: work out is positive."),
      P("Find the heat needed to raise 2 kg of liquid water from 20 °C to 100 °C in a boiler (cp ≈ 4.18 kJ/kg·K).", "Q = mCpΔT = 2×4.18×80 = 668.8 kJ", "Sensible heating of liquid water precedes boiling.", "Using the specific heat of liquid water about 4.18 kJ/kg·K, Q = mCp(T₂ − T₁) = 2 × 4.18 × 80 = 668.8 kJ. This is the sensible (no phase-change) preheat; boiling would subsequently require the latent heat h_fg at 100 °C."),
      P("What is the latent heat of vaporization at the critical point?", "Zero", "Phases merge at the critical point so no latent heat remains.", "It is zero; at the critical point there is no distinction between liquid and vapour. Hence the vaporization curve ends there, and above T_c the fluid is a single supercritical phase. In supercritical boilers, no constant-temperature boiling zone exists; the water transitions continuously into steam-like fluid."),
      P("What two fixed points originally defined the Celsius scale?", "Ice point (0 °C) and steam point (100 °C) at 101.325 kPa", "Modern temperature scales are defined via the triple point.", "Historically, the Celsius scale was fixed by the ice point (0 °C, melting of ice) and the steam point (100 °C, boiling water) at standard atmospheric pressure, divided into 100 equal intervals. Modern metrology instead uses the triple point of water (0.01 °C) as the single defining point."),
      P("A Carnot heat pump delivers 5 kW of heat at 300 K while drawing heat from a 270 K source. Find the power input.", "Q_H/W = T_H/(T_H − T_L) = 300/30 = 10, so W = 5/10 = 0.5 kW", "COP_HP = T_H/(T_H − T_L).", "The maximum heat-pump COP between 270 K and 300 K is COP_HP = 300/(300 − 270) = 10, so the minimum power input is W = Q_H/COP = 5/10 = 0.5 kW. Real heat pumps would consume more due to compressor irreversibilities."),
      P("Find the entropy change of the universe when 100 kJ flows from 500 K to 300 K.", "ΔS = Q(1/T_L − 1/T_H) = 100(1/300 − 1/500) = 100×0.001333 ≈ 0.133 kJ/K", "Heat transfer across finite ΔT is irreversible.", "ΔS_universe = −Q/T_H + Q/T_L = Q(1/T_L − 1/T_H) = 100 × (0.003333 − 0.0020) = 0.133 kJ/K. Since it is positive, the spontaneous heat transfer is irreversible and creates entropy. A smaller temperature gap would reduce the entropy production."),
      P("What is the key difference between a condenser and a boiler thermodynamically?", "Boiler adds heat at high pressure (vaporizes); condenser removes heat at low pressure (condenses)", "Both are constant-pressure heat exchangers in the Rankine cycle.", "In the Rankine cycle the boiler supplies heat at the high pressure, converting feedwater into (usually superheated) steam, while the condenser rejects heat at the low back pressure, condensing exhaust steam to saturated liquid. Both processes are idealized as constant-pressure, and each is one of the four cycle devices."),
      P("Define the polytropic index that makes a reversible process neither gain nor lose heat.", "n = γ", "Then Cn = 0 and PV^γ = constant.", "The polytropic specific heat is Cn = Cv(n − γ)/(n − 1); it vanishes when n = γ. Then the process is adiabatic (Q = 0) and reversible, i.e., isentropic. This is why the adiabatic exponent γ is sometimes called the isentropic index k."),
      P("Why does an isothermal expansion produce the maximum work for expansion between two volumes at constant temperature?", "The product P dV is integrated with P held at its highest value at every point", "Lower pressure would give less area under the curve.", "For a given volume change at constant temperature the ideal-gas isotherm P = mRT/V keeps the pressure at the largest possible value it can have at each volume; any cooling below T would give a smaller P and hence a smaller area (work). Among polytropic expansions from V₁ to V₂, no path gives more work than n = 1 at fixed T."),
      P("What is the relation between entropy and the heat of phase transformation?", "ΔS_phase = Δh_phase/T_phase", "Applies to fusion, vaporization, and sublimation.", "For any reversible phase change at the transition temperature T, the entropy change is Δs = Δh_phase/T, e.g., s_fg = h_fg/T_sat for evaporation. This is used with steam tables to locate wet states and is also how the saturation curve slope is derived via Clapeyron's equation."),
      P("What is the numerical value of the standard molar volume of any ideal gas at 0 °C and 101.325 kPa?", "22.414 m³ per kmol (22.414 litres per mole)", "Avogadro volume from V = RuT/P.", "Using V = RuT/P = 8.314 × 273.15/101.325 ≈ 22.414 m³/kmol. Because the gas constant Ru is universal, every ideal gas shares this molar volume at a given P and T. This is the basis of Avogadro's law and of normal-hexane-type gas calculations."),
      P("What is the principle of the separation of moisture in steam by a separator?", "Momentum and inertia separate dense droplets from light vapour", "Baffles, cyclones, and gravity are used.", "A steam separator forces the wet flow through changes of direction; the heavier water droplets, lacking the vapour's ability to turn, impact the walls and fall while the dry steam flows on. This inertia-based separation is exactly what a separating calorimeter exploits to find dryness fraction."),
      P("Convert 100 °F to Celsius.", "°C = (5/9)(100 − 32) = (5/9)×68 ≈ 37.8 °C", "Use °C = (F − 32)/1.8.", "°C = (5/9)(°F − 32) = (5/9)(100 − 32) = 37.78 °C. Conversion checks: 32 °F = 0 °C and 212 °F = 100 °C. Such unit conversions are standard in SSC-JE temperature-scale questions."),
      P("What is the specific heat ratio of monatomic helium?", "γ ≈ 1.667 (5/3)", "Monatomic ideal gas: Cp/Cv = (5/2)/(3/2).", "For a monatomic ideal gas, molar specific heats are Cv = (3/2)Ru and Cp = (5/2)Ru, so γ = 5/3 ≈ 1.667. Helium's high γ and large R per kg make it a working fluid for high-temperature gas cycles and a candidate for regenerated Brayton plants."),
      P("A rigid tank contains air. It is heated so its pressure doubles. Find the ratio of final to initial temperature.", "T₂/T₁ = P₂/P₁ = 2 (constant-volume heating)", "Ideal gas with V fixed.", "For a rigid tank V is constant, so the gas law gives T₂/T₁ = P₂/P₁ = 2. The internal energy rises accordingly: Δu = Cv(T₂ − T₁) = 0.718 × T₁ per kg, and no work is done."),
      P("Explain how a throttling calorimeter measures the enthalpy of saturated steam entering it.", "It throttles the sample and reads the superheated exit state", "Conserved enthalpy h₁ = h₂ gives the inlet quality.", "In a throttling calorimeter, the wet steam accelerates through an orifice into a much lower pressure where it becomes superheated. Measuring that low-pressure exit temperature and pressure fixes h₂; because the process is isenthalpic, the inlet enthalpy equals h₂. Comparing with h_f + x·h_fg returns the quality."),
      P("What is the difference between gauge pressure and absolute pressure?", "Absolute = gauge + atmospheric; gauge is measured above atmosphere", "Va cuum pressure is atmosphere minus absolute.", "Absolute pressure is measured relative to a perfect vacuum; gauge pressure is measured relative to atmospheric pressure: P_abs = P_gauge + P_atm. A vacuum is the deficit below atmospheric, P_vacuum = P_atm − P_abs. All gas-law and thermodynamic formulas must use absolute pressures."),
      P("Convert a gauge pressure of 5 bar to absolute pressure at sea level.", "P_abs = 5 + 1.013 = 6.013 bar", "Add standard atmospheric pressure 1.01325 bar.", "P_abs = P_gauge + P_atmosphere = 5 + 1.01325 = 6.01325 bar ≈ 601.3 kPa. Thermodynamic property tables require absolute values, so mariners and engineers must always add the atmospheric contribution when reading a pressure gauge."),
      P("What is the name of the process in which the difference of two equilibrium states is a vertical line on the T-s diagram?", "Isentropic process", "On the T-s plane vertical lines are constant-s lines.", "A vertical (constant-entropy) path on the T-s diagram is an isentropic process, which is reversible and adiabatic. Turbine and compressor ideal processes are traced as vertical lines, and the horizontal distance between the end states equals the ideal shaft work. No vertical-line path can cross the saturation dome without phase change physics."),
      P("Explain why the Carnot engine is an idealization.", "Its four processes require infinite time and zero driving differences", "Isothermal heat transfer needs zero ΔT and hence an infinite area.", "The Carnot cycle demands reversible heat transfer, which requires infinitesimal temperature differences and hence infinite time and heat-exchange area, plus frictionless adiabatic steps with negligible imbalances. These conditions cannot be met in hardware. Nevertheless, its efficiency sets the absolute upper bound for real engines."),
      P("What is the mathematical definition of temperature?", "1/T = (∂S/∂U)_V", "Definition for a closed simple compressible system.", "Temperature is defined thermodynamically as 1/T = (∂S/∂U)_V, the change in entropy with internal energy at constant volume. This definition is independent of any particular thermometer and follows from the combined first/second-law statement T dS = dU + P dV. It makes T positive and establishes the absolute (Kelvin) scale."),
      P("Water boils at 100 °C at standard pressure. What is its boiling temperature if the pressure is reduced by a vacuum pump?", "Lower than 100 °C", "Lower ambient pressure lowers the saturation temperature.", "Saturation temperature falls with pressure: at 50 kPa water boils near 81.3 °C, and at 10 kPa near 45.8 °C. This is why food cooks faster in pressurized vessels and why water can be boiled at low temperature on mountains. The Clausius–Clapeyron relation quantifies the drop."),
      P("A steam turbine receives steam at 3000 kPa and 300 °C and exhausts at 50 kPa. How would you find the actual work per kg, given η_t = 0.85?", "Actual w = η_t(h₁ − h₂s), where h₂s is read at 50 kPa on the constant-s line", "First find the isentropic exit enthalpy from the Mollier chart or tables.", "Read h₁ from the superheated tables at 3 MPa, 300 °C. Locate the exit state at 50 kPa with s₂ = s₁ (isentropic) and read h₂s. The ideal work is h₁ − h₂s, and the actual work is w_actual = η_t(h₁ − h₂s) = 0.85(h₁ − h₂s). Then the actual exit enthalpy is h₂ = h₁ − w_actual."),
      P("What does a 0.9 dryness fraction mean in a wet steam line?", "90% of the mass is vapour and 10% is liquid", "x = m_g/(m_f + m_g) = 0.9.", "Quality x = 0.9 means nine-tenths of the mixture mass is saturated vapour while one-tenth is liquid droplets. Its enthalpy is h = hf + 0.9·hfg and its specific volume v ≈ 0.9·v_g. In turbines such wet states are avoided near the last stages where drops erode blades."),
      P("Which law is used to explain that a thermometer reads the temperature of what it touches?", "The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics", "Thermal equilibrium is transitive.", "The zeroth law allows a third body (the thermometer) that reaches thermal equilibrium with a sample to share the sample's temperature, since equilibrium is transitive. It thus gives operational meaning to temperature measurement and underlies every thermometer reading."),
      P("Express the first law for a cyclic steady-flow device such as a pump working on the fluid.", "For the fluid: h₂ − h₁ = w_pump (input) with no heat/KE/PE changes", "SFEE with Q̇ = 0.", "Applying the SFEE to the pump (adiabatic, negligible KE/PE) gives w_pump = h₂ − h₁ for the fluid; the pump supplies this work. Because the liquid is nearly incompressible, w_pump ≈ v_f(P₂ − P₁). This enters the Rankine cycle as the small pump-work term subtracted from the turbine work."),
      P("Two identical Carnot engines operate in series between 900 K and 300 K. If the intermediate temperature is 600 K, find the total efficiency.", "η_total = 1 − T_L/T_H = 1 − 300/900 ≈ 0.667 regardless of staging", "Cascading Carnot engines has no gain over a single one.", "For cascaded reversible engines the combined efficiency equals that of a single Carnot engine between the same extremes: η = 1 − 300/900 = 2/3. This is readily verified since Q_H/Q_M = T_H/T_M and Q_M/Q_L = T_M/T_L give Q_H/Q_L = T_H/T_L; the intermediate temperature cancels."),
      P("What is a condensate pump and where is it placed?", "It lifts saturated condensate from the hotwell back to the feed system", "Located downstream of the condenser hotwell.", "The condensate pump takes the condensate from the condenser hotwell and pushes it through the low-pressure feedwater heaters toward the boiler. Its work, like all pump work in the Rankine cycle, is small because the liquid is nearly incompressible. A separate boiler feed pump then raises pressure further to the boiler."),
      P("What is the effect of increasing the number of regenerative feedwater stages?", "Efficiency improves but with diminishing returns", "Each added heater raises the average heat-addition temperature slightly less.", "Each extraction point raises the feedwater temperature a bit closer to the boiler saturation temperature, increasing the average temperature of heat addition and hence efficiency. The gain per stage shrinks, and cost and complexity rise, so plants use an optimum (typically 6–8 feedwater-heating stages for large steam cycles)."),
      P("How is the Reynolds analogy or dimensional reasoning used in thermodynamics? (Give a thermodynamic use of dimensionless numbers.)", "Groups like Re, Pr, Nu correlate convective heat transfer and friction", "Thermodynamics provides energy balances; heat-transfer correlations use dimensionless numbers.", "In heat-exchanger and boiler sizing, convective coefficients come from correlations Nu = f(Re, Pr), e.g., Nu = 0.023 Re^0.8 Pr^0.4 for turbulent pipe flow. These dimensionless numbers link the flow field to the heat transfer that the thermodynamic cycle analysis supplies. This integration is standard in power-plant design."),
      P("What does the T-ds equation yield for a constant-pressure heating process?", "ds = Cp dT/T for an ideal gas", "Set dP = 0 in T ds = dh − v dP.", "Setting dP = 0 in T ds = dh − v dP gives T ds = dh = Cp dT, so ds = Cp dT/T. Integrating from T₁ to T₂ gives Δs = Cp ln(T₂/T₁), the constant-pressure entropy change. This is the derivation behind the simplest entropy formula used in air-standard problems."),
      P("What is a gas turbine regenerator?", "A counter-flow heat exchanger recovering exhaust heat to preheat combustor-inlet air", "It reduces fuel input and raises efficiency when T₄ > T₂.", "A regenerator (recuperator) transfers heat from the turbine exhaust to the compressor discharge air before combustion. The fuel needed for 2→3 is then smaller, raising the cycle efficiency. The improvement is largest at low pressure ratios, where T₄ − T₂ is greatest; at high r_p the effect shrinks or vanishes."),
      P("Find the heat rejected by a Carnot refrigerator that consumes 1 kW while keeping a space at 250 K, given T_H = 300 K.", "Q_L = W·COP = 1×5 = 5 kW (COP = 250/50 = 5)", "Q_H = Q_L + W = 6 kW.", "COP_Carnot = T_L/(T_H − T_L) = 250/50 = 5, so the heat removed is Q_L = W·COP = 1 × 5 = 5 kW. The heat rejected to the hot side is Q_H = Q_L + W = 6 kW. Real refrigerators reject more for the same 1 kW due to irreversibility."),
      P("Define the specific heat of a substance in general.", "c = δQ/(m dT) for a specified process", "Common values are Cp and Cv for constant-P and constant-V processes.", "Specific heat is the heat needed per unit mass and unit temperature rise for a specified process: c = δQ/(m dT). Since Q is a path function, any c depends on the path; Cp = (∂h/∂T)_P and Cv = (∂u/∂T)_V are the two standard forms, and for ideal gases Cp − Cv = R."),
      P("What is the standard boiling point of water on the Kelvin scale?", "373.15 K", "100 °C ≈ 373.15 K at 101.325 kPa.", "Water boils at 100 °C, and 100 °C = 373.15 K. On the Rankine scale that is about 671.67 °R. The constant-pressure boiling temperature is fixed by the saturation pressure, vanishing at the critical point where the boiling plateau disappears."),
      P("Air is compressed from 100 kPa, 300 K to 2000 kPa polytropically with n = 1.35. Find the exit temperature.", "T₂ = 300 × 20^(0.35/1.35) = 300 × 20^0.2593", "Compute 20^0.2593 ≈ 2.17 → T₂ ≈ 651 K.", "For a polytropic process T₂/T₁ = (P₂/P₁)^((n−1)/n) = 20^(0.35/1.35) = 20^0.2593. Since 20^0.2593 ≈ 2.17, T₂ ≈ 300 × 2.17 ≈ 651 K. This is close to, but slightly above, the isentropic value because n = 1.35 < γ = 1.4 adds a little heat during compression."),
      P("A rigid tank containing air at 300 K is charged with more air. Why does the analysis use conservation of mass with the first law?", "The open-system (control volume) energy and mass balances must be combined", "An unsteady charging problem is solved with m₂u₂ − m₁u₁ = Σ Q − Σ W + Σ mₑh.", "Charging a rigid tank is an unsteady flow problem: mass enters (m₂ = m₁ + mᵢ) while volume is fixed. Its energy balance is m₂u₂ − m₁u₁ = mᵢhᵢ (with Q = W = 0). Using u = CvT and h = CpT, the final temperature comes out higher than an isochoric estimate would give, illustrating SFEE's control-volume discipline."),
      P("What is meant by the triple-point pressure of water in mm of mercury?", "4.58 mm Hg (≈ 611.657 Pa at 0 °C ice point)", "mm-Hg unit: 1 mm Hg ≈ 133.32 Pa.", "The triple-point pressure of water, 611.657 Pa, corresponds to about 4.58 mm Hg. Because this point uniquely fixes the thermodynamic state, it is used to calibrate thermometers and to define the kelvin. It is far below standard atmospheric pressure."),
      P("Write the steady-flow energy equation for a process with negligible kinetic and potential terms.", "h₁ + q = h₂ + w", "Per unit mass, with q and w specific energies.", "With KE and PE changes neglected, the SFEE reduces to h₁ + q = h₂ + w per unit mass, where q is heat added and w is work done by the fluid. Solving w = h₁ − h₂ + q recovers the boiler (q —), turbine (w —), compressor, nozzle, and throttle (q = w = 0) special cases with ease."),
      P("Why are the Carnot statements of the two laws written as negatives (impossibilities)?", "The laws state what cannot be achieved, not what can", "Both are exclusions of perpetual-motion machines.", "Thermodynamics' fundamental laws are assertion of impossibility: no machine can create energy (first law) and no cyclic engine can fully convert heat to work (second law). Stating impossibilities absolutes has proven robust empirically, whereas positive rule-claims would require enumerating all machines. The corollaries, like all engines are worse than Carnot, then follow directly."),
      P("What is the difference between a closed feedwater heater drain and an open-type trap end?", "Closed heater drains are throttled or cascaded to lower-pressure heaters", "Open heaters mix drains directly; closed heaters handle drain streams separately.", "In closed feedwater heaters, the extraction-condensate (drain) leaves at heater pressure and is either throttled (trapped) forward to the next-lower heater or pumped into the feed line. In an open heater, extraction steam condenses directly into the water, so a distinct drain stream and its associated throttling losses are largely eliminated at the cost of an extra pump."),
      P("A gas expands polytropically with n = γ/2 from 400 K to 200 K. Find the heat transfer per kg (Cv = 0.718, R = 0.287).", "Q = Cv(n−γ)/(n−1)(T₂−T₁); (0.5γ−γ)/(0.5γ−1) = (−0.5γ)/(0.5γ−1) = (−0.7)/(−0.3) = 7/3; Q = 0.718×2.333×(−200) ≈ −335 kJ/kg", "Polytropic specific heat Cn from formula; heat leaves.", "With n = γ/2 = 0.7, Cn = Cv(n−γ)/(n−1) = 0.718 × (0.7−1.4)/(0.7−1) = 0.718 × 2.333 ≈ 1.675 kJ/kgK. Then Q = Cn(T₂ − T₁) = 1.675 × (200 − 400) ≈ −335 kJ/kg (heat rejected). The work meanwhile is W = R(T₁−T₂)/(n−1) = 0.287×200/0.3 ≈ 191 kJ/kg out."),
      P("How does the mean effective pressure relate to the brake mean effective pressure?", "BMEP = MEP × (indicated/brake) efficiency, or BMEP = 60P_brake/(n·V_s·N)", "Friction reduces brake MEP below indicated MEP.", "Indicated MEP follows from the indicator diagram: IMEP = W_indicated/V_displacement. Friction between the piston, rings, and bearings converts some work to heat, so BMEP = IMEP − FMEP (friction MEP). Brake power and BMEP relate by the engine speed and stroke geometry; the ratio BMEP/IMEP is the mechanical efficiency."),
      P("What is the Cp of superheated steam approximately?", "Near 2.08 kJ/kg·K", "Steam's Cp is far above air's because water has more degrees of freedom.", "At ordinary superheat conditions the specific heat of steam at constant pressure is nearly 2.08 kJ/kg·K, roughly double that of air. It varies with temperature and pressure, and steam tables remain the correct source for enthalpy changes; the approximate Cp is handy for quick superheat enthalpy estimates in nozzles and turbines."),
      P("Summarize the second law as it applies to the mixing of two different ideal gases.", "Spontaneous mixing is irreversible and increases entropy", "ΔS_mix = −R Σ y ln y per mole > 0.", "When two distinct ideal gases mix at constant T and P, the entropy increases by ΔS_mix = −R Σ yᵢ ln yᵢ per mole, which is positive because each yᵢ < 1. Undoing the mixing would require work, so the mixing is irreversible. This entropy-of-mixing analysis guides separation costs in gas processing."),
      P("What is the specific enthalpy of saturated steam at 10 MPa? (Indicate the table search.)", "Read h_g from saturated tables at 10 MPa ≈ 2724.9 kJ/kg", "At 10 MPa, h_f ≈ 1407.6 kJ/kg and h_fg ≈ 1317.3 kJ/kg.", "At 10 MPa saturation, the tables give h_f ≈ 1407.6 kJ/kg and h_fg ≈ 1317.3 kJ/kg, so h_g = h_f + h_fg ≈ 2724.9 kJ/kg, with s_g ≈ 5.614 kJ/kgK and v_g ≈ 0.018026 m³/kg. Wet states at this pressure are found by interpolation with quality."),
      P("Why is flash steam analysis relevant in high-pressure condensate draining?", "High-pressure condensate flashes to wet steam when throttled", "Throttling is isenthalpic and produces flash steam.", "Hot condensate at boiler/feed pressure, when throttled through traps to lower pressure, releases a fraction of flash steam because its enthalpy exceeds h_f at the lower saturation pressure. The flash fraction is computed from the isenthalpic constraint x = (h_1 − h_f2)/h_fg2. Proper flash recovery improves plant efficiency by reclaiming the flash steam."),
      P("A sink of 300 K absorbs the entire heat rejected by a 1000 K hot reservoir in a 400 kJ-work process. Find Q_H for η = 40%.", "η = W/Q_H → Q_H = 400/0.40 = 1000 kJ, Q_L = 600 kJ", "Carnot check: Q_L/Q_H = 0.6 = T_L/T_H ✓.", "If the engine's thermal efficiency is 0.40, the heat added is Q_H = W/η = 400/0.40 = 1000 kJ and Q_L = Q_H − W = 600 kJ. The reversible (Carnot) limit between 1000 K and 300 K is 70%, so this engine's efficiency is well below the ideal — entropy is being generated."),
      P("What is a reasonable maximum efficiency of a simple single-shaft gas-turbine plant and of a combined cycle?", "Simple cycle ≈ 30–40%, combined cycle ≈ 55–62%", "Modern heavy-duty turbines approach 42–44% simple and 60%+ combined.", "A simple-cycle industrial gas turbine achieves roughly 30–40% LHV efficiency (advanced aeroderivatives to 44%). Because the exhaust still contains much energy, a bottoming steam Rankine cycle recovers it and lifts the plant to about 55–62%. This is the highest efficiency available among thermal power technologies."),
      P("What is the phase of water at 0.01 °C, 611.657 Pa?", "Ice, liquid, and vapour coexist (triple point)", "A single unique state where all three phases meet.", "At exactly 0.01 °C (273.16 K) and 611.657 Pa, all three phases of pure water coexist in equilibrium. This is the triple point. Perturbing T or P leaves the sample in a single phase or a two-phase mix; the triple point is the zero-degree-of-freedom state, exploited to define the kelvin."),
      P("A mixture contains 2 kg N₂ and 1 kg O₂. Find the mixture gas constant.", "n_N2 = 2000/28.013 ≈ 71.4 mol, n_O2 = 1000/32 ≈ 31.25 mol; R_mix = 8.314×(71.4+31.25)/3000 ≈ 0.284 kJ/kg·K", "R_mix = Ru·(total moles)/mass.", "Total moles ≈ 102.65, total mass = 3 kg, so the mixture constant is R_mix = Ru·n_total/m_total = 8.314 × 102.65/3 ≈ 0.284 kJ/kg·K, very close to pure air's 0.287. The molar-weighted computation matches a mass-weighted average of R_N₂ and R_O₂."),
      P("What curve separates the compressed-liquid region from the two-phase region on a P-v diagram?", "The saturated-liquid line", "x = 0 line.", "The saturated-liquid (x = 0) line marks the boundary between the compressed liquid and the two-phase region on a P-v (and T-s, h-s) diagram. To its left, liquid water is compressed (subcooled); to the right within the dome, a wet mixture exists. The saturated-vapour (x = 1) line is the other boundary of the dome."),
      P("At what value of temperature does the entropy of S, u, h of water vanish?", "At absolute zero (0 K) by the third law", "Also imprecise values are irrelevant for engineering energy balances.", "The third law states that the entropy of a perfectly ordered crystal at absolute zero is zero, S(0 K) = 0. Internal energy and enthalpy are defined relative to arbitrary references (e.g., enthalpy of saturated liquid at 0.01 °C ≈ 0 in steam tables), so only differences matter; entropy alone has an absolute zero point."),
      P("A tank has air at 200 kPa, 300 K. A leak slowly discharges the air isothermally. What is the control-volume energy balance simplification?", "d(mu)/dt = −ṁh (no heat, no work, well-insulated)", "The exiting enthalpy h is C pT since flow energy leaves.", "For a well-insulated rigid tank leaking slowly at constant temperature, the CV energy balance is d(mu)/dt = −ṁh_e. Using u = C_v T and h = C_p T with T constant gives a relation linking m and T times; the mathematics shows the tank must cool unless heat is supplied, a classic transient-flow result."),
      P("Why do pointed questions about steam-table interpolation test readiness for SSC-JE?", "Interpolation is needed to read properties between table rows", "Linear interpolation holds nearly exactly between neighboring rows.", "Steam tables list properties at discrete temperatures or pressures; a state between rows requires linear interpolation, e.g., h at 105 °C from rows at 100 °C and 110 °C. Since properties vary smoothly, linear interpolation to one decimal is standard. This skill is essential for Rankine, nozzle, and turbine problems."),
      P("What is the exact saturation pressure of water at its triple point?", "611.657 Pa", "Also 4.58 mm Hg or 0.006112 bar.", "The triple point of water has an exact defined saturation pressure of 611.657 Pa (6.11657 mbar). Because it coexists with ice, it is used to calibrate pressure standards and vacuum gauges via the equilibrium principle. It is the lowest fixed pressure for defining the Kelvin scale."),
      P("Bypass an equal-diameter question and state the convention of sign for work in turbines.", "Work done BY the system is positive", "Ẇ > 0 for a power-producing machine.", "The standard engineering convention takes work done by the system (output) as positive. A turbine therefore has positive Ẇ = ṁ(h₁ − h₂), while a compressor, which requires input, has negative work in the same sign frame. Consistency of signs prevents errors in SFEE problems."),
      P("What polytropic index is often recommended for practical air-compression calculations?", "n ≈ 1.3", "Compression with some cooling lies between isothermal (1.0) and isentropic (1.4).", "Because cylinder walls carry away heat, real reciprocating air compression follows a polytropic path with n around 1.2–1.35 instead of the isentropic 1.4. The value 1.3 is the standard textbook choice. It yields work and temperature between the isothermal and isentropic extremes, and it is the most used value in compressor problems."),
      P("What is the difference between a counter-flow and a parallel-flow heat exchanger in thermodynamic terms?", "Counter-flow gives the larger log-mean temperature difference and heat transfer", "T_LMTD, counterflow ≥ T_LMTD, parallel.", "In the counter-flow arrangement the hot and cold streams move in opposite directions, producing the largest possible temperature difference profile and hence the highest heat transfer per unit area and the smallest entropy generation for a fixed duty. Parallel flow is simpler but delivers less heat and more entropy generated. The LMTD formula distinguishes them."),
      P("How is the dryness fraction of wet steam defined thermodynamically without a calorimeter?", "x = (s − sf)/sfg = (h − hf)/hfg = (v − vf)/v_fg", "Three equivalent routes from property tables give the same x.", "Once one property of the wet state is measured (usually T or P plus one of h, s, or v), quality follows by linear interpolation inside the dome: x = (h − hf)/hfg etc. Because all properties are functions of x, consistent tables give identical results. This is the basis used in steam-turbine wetness problems."),
      P("What typical efficiency does a modern industrial steam-turbine plant attain?", "35–42%", "Supercritical plants reach about 42–45%.", "A modern subcritical coal-fired Rankine plant converts about 35–40% of fuel LHV into electricity; supercritical designs push to 42–45%, and ultra-supercritical plus reheating approaches around 45–47%. These numbers remain far below the Carnot limit because of finite-ΔT heat transfer everywhere."),
      P("In exergy terms, why is low-temperature heat low-grade?", "Its exergy content T₀/T − 1 is small", "Heat availability Q(1 − T_L/T_H) falls rapidly with source temperature.", "The exergy (available part) of heat Q released at temperature T is X = Q(1 − T₀/T), so heat delivered near the environment temperature T₀ carries almost no work potential and is high entropy. This grading underlies cogeneration value: high-temperature heat is used for power first, then lower-temperature heat for processes."),
      P("What is the Joule–Thomson effect when μJT < 0?", "Throttling WARMS the gas (inversion is crossed)", "Above the inversion temperature heating occurs.", "When μJT < 0, a pressure drop during isenthalpic throttling raises the temperature, which happens above the inversion temperature. Warm fluids like air above about 603 K and most gases far from liquefaction conditions exhibit this. It explains why throttling is not always a cooling process."),
      P("Give the approximate molar mass and gas constant of air.", "M ≈ 28.97 kg/kmol, R ≈ 0.287 kJ/kg·K", "Air is 78% N₂, 21% O₂ by volume.", "Air's molar mass follows from M = Σ yᵢMᵢ = 0.78×28.013 + 0.21×32.00 + 0.01×39.95 ≈ 28.97 kg/kmol. Then R = Ru/M = 8.314/28.97 ≈ 0.287 kJ/kg·K and γ = 1.4 at room temperature. These numbers anchor nearly every SSC-JE gas problem."),
      P("How is the Otto cycle drawn on P-v and T-s planes?", "P-v: two verticals and two adiabatic curves; T-s: two horizontal isobars inside dome plus two tilted isentropes", "The enclosed areas give the net work.", "On the P-v diagram the Otto cycle's constant-volume processes are vertical segments and its isentropes are steep curves, enclosing the net work. On the T-s plane heat addition appears as a left-to-right stroke at increasing constants and heat rejection as a downward constant-volume line back along the same volume path; the area between is W_net = mCv[(T₃−T₂)−(T₄−T₁)]."),
      P("What is the representative efficiency of an Otto engine at r = 8 (γ = 1.4)?", "η = 1 − 8^(−0.4) ≈ 0.565 (56.5%)", "8^0.4 ≈ 2.297.", "η = 1 − 1/r^(γ−1) = 1 − 1/8^0.4 = 1 − 1/2.297 ≈ 0.565. Increasing r to 10 would give ≈ 0.602, but the gains per unit r shrink and knock limits intervene, so engines rarely exceed about r = 12 on gasoline."),
      P("What is the representative efficiency of a Diesel engine at r = 18, r_c = 2 (γ = 1.4)?", "η = 1 − (1/18^0.4)×[(2^1.4−1)/(1.4×(2−1))] = 1 − (1/3.18)×(1.639/1.4)", "Compute 18^0.4 ≈ 3.18; bracket ≈ 1.171; η ≈ 1 − 0.368 ≈ 0.632.", "η = 1 − (1/r^(γ−1))·[(r_c^γ − 1)/(γ(r_c − 1))] = 1 − (1/3.18)·[(2^1.4−1)/(1.4)] = 1 − 0.314·(1.639/1.4) = 1 − 0.368 ≈ 0.632. The higher compression ratio compensates for the cutoff-ratio penalty, making the Diesel engine competitive."),
      P("Compare these efficiencies and the point of Cycles comparison: Otto at r=8 and Diesel at r=18.", "Otto 56.5% versus Diesel ≈ 63.2%", "Diesel's advantage arises solely from its higher compression ratio.", "This concrete pair illustrates the standard comparison: at the SAME r the Otto wins, but a Diesel engine may run at far higher r (18 vs 8) because it compresses air alone, avoiding knock. Its efficiency then beats the Otto at lower r. Compression ratio, not cycle type alone, decides comparison results."),
      P("What is the mean effective pressure for the idealized Otto at r = 8, P₁ = 100 kPa, T₁ = 300 K and q_in = 1800 kJ/kg?", "First T₂ = 300×8^0.4 ≈ 689.4 K; q_in = Cv(T₃−T₂) → T₃ = 689.4+1800/0.718 ≈ 3196 K; W_net = η q = 0.565×1800 ≈ 1017 kJ/kg; v_max−v_min = RT₁(1−1/r)/P₁ = 0.287×300×0.875/100 ≈ 0.7532 m³/kg; MEP = 1017/0.7532 ≈ 1350 kPa", "Compute methodically; MEP ≈ 13.5 bar.", "Using the ideal-gas relation T₂ = T₁ r^(γ−1) = 300 × 8^0.4 ≈ 689.4 K, then T₃ = T₂ + q_in/Cv ≈ 3196 K. The net work W_net = η q_in ≈ 0.565 × 1800 ≈ 1017 kJ/kg. Displacement volume v₁ − v₂ = (RT₁/P₁)(1 − 1/r) = (0.287×300/100)(0.875) ≈ 0.753 m³/kg. Hence MEP = 1017/0.753 ≈ 1350 kPa. Formula: MEP = W_net/(v₁ − v₂)."),
      P("What does the term subcooled (compressed) liquid mean in steam tables?", "A liquid state below saturation at the given pressure", "Temperature below T_sat; tables list h ≈ h_f at the same T.", "A compressed liquid has T < T_sat(P). Its properties are approximated by the saturated-liquid values at the same temperature, h(T,P) ≈ h_f(T), because the pressure effect on liquid enthalpy is minute. This approximation is used for feedwater and boiler-inlet states where pressures are high."),
      P("How is the reversible work of a Carnot heat pump used as a bound in engineering?", "Any real heat pump needs more work than this", "COP_real ≤ COP_Carnot.", "The Carnot COP, COP = T_H/(T_H − T_L) for a heat pump or T_L/(T_H − T_L) for a refrigerator, sets the theoretical ceiling. A validated heat-pump cycle delivers Q_H/W only up to that ceiling; the ratio of actual to Carnot COP is the second-law efficiency. Designers use it to identify where investments give the best improvements."),
      P("What is the principleof operation of a refrigerator cycle in thermodynamic terms?", "It absorbs heat from the low-temperature space, does work, and rejects heat at ambient", "COP_R = Q_L/W.", "A vapour-compression refrigerator evaporates refrigerant in the cold space (absorbing Q_L), compresses the vapour, condenses it at ambient (rejecting Q_H = Q_L + W), and throttles the liquid back. The COP Q_L/W typically ranges from 2.5 to 5. Its idealization is the reversed Carnot cycle with T_L and T_H being the evaporator and condenser temperatures."),
      P("State why two bodies in contact exchange heat until the same temperature.", "Thermal equilibrium is a minimum-free-energy, maximum-entropy state", "Heat flows until temperatures equalize.", "When two bodies at different temperatures exchange heat, entropy increases until an equilibrium is reached, and no further spontaneous exchange can occur; equilibrium coincides with equal temperatures at constant volume. Since dS/dU determines 1/T (the definition of temperature), equality of temperature means no driving potential for further heat flow."),
      P("Convert the triple point of water to °F.", "0.01 °C → 32.018 °F", "Use °F = (9/5)×0.01 + 32.", "°F = (9/5)×°C + 32 = 1.8×0.01 + 32 = 32.018 °F. The triple-point temperature is then 273.16 K, 0.01 °C, or 32.018 °F, and 491.688 °R. This single state provides the Kelvin-scale anchor regardless of the thermometer material."),
      P("A reversible engine absorbs 2000 kJ at 800 K and rejects at 400 K; find work and efficiency.", "η = 1 − 400/800 = 0.5; W = 1000 kJ; Q_L = 1000 kJ", "Carnot between 800 K and 400 K.", "η_Carnot = 1 − T_L/T_H = 1 − 400/800 = 0.5. The work output is W = η·Q_H = 0.5 × 2000 = 1000 kJ, and the rejected heat Q_L = Q_H − W = 1000 kJ, which equals Q_H·T_L/T_H = 2000 × 0.5 exactly."),
      P("Give the steady-flow equation for a calorically perfect gas turbine stage.", "w_t = Cp(T₁ − T₂), and with T₂ = T₁·(P₂/P₁)^((γ−1)/γ) for the ideal", "Apply to the isentropic nozzle/stator and rotor.", "For a stage treated as a perfect gas with constant Cp and adiabatic conditions, w_t = Cp(T₁ − T₂). The isentropic relation T₂ = T₁(P₂/P₁)^((γ−1)/γ) gives the ideal work. Actual stages multiply this by the stage efficiency and suffer profile, cooling, and clearance losses — the basis of stage-stacking calculations in gas-turbine design."),
      P("What is the principle of the psychrometric chart in moist air?", "At a total pressure, air-vapour mixture states are located by T, φ, and humidity ratio", "Psychrometry is a special ideal-mixture thermodynamics application.", "Moist air is a two-component ideal mixture (dry air + water vapour). The psychrometric chart fixes states by dry-bulb temperature, relative humidity, and humidity ratio using Dalton's law at a fixed total pressure. Energy and mass balances on coils and towers then follow from the mixture enthalpy and moisture content, applying the very mixture laws used for ideal gases."),
      P("How does the SDJ (second day of a job) analogy, the Carnot-Clausius relation, define thermodynamic temperature regardless of the working fluid?", "The ratio Q_H/Q_L equals the reservoir temperature ratio T_H/T_L", "Absolute temperature is defined so this ratio is simple.", "Thermodynamics defines absolute temperature by insisting that the heat ratio of reversible engines equals the temperature ratio: Q_H/Q_L = T_H/T_L. This is Kelvin's postulate; it makes efficiency η = 1 − Q_L/Q_H = 1 − T_L/T_H independent of any working substance. The triple point of water then anchors the scale's numerical value."),
    ];
  if (typeof module !== "undefined" && module.exports) module.exports = SSC_JE_ENC_THERMO;
  if (typeof window !== "undefined") window.SSC_JE_ENC_THERMO = SSC_JE_ENC_THERMO;
})();